WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:09.720
- Welcome everyone, and
thank you for coming.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:10.860
So, I'm Rohini Pande.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:12.450
I'm a Professor of Economics,

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.820
and the Director of the
Economic Growth Center.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.910
I also, together with
Dr. Charity Troyer Moore,

00:00:17.910 --> 00:00:20.583
direct the Inclusion
Economics at Yale University.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.690
Inclusion Economics is a policy
engaged research initiative

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:27.510
which seeks to promote
inclusive institutions,

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:29.250
economies, and societies.

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:30.360
So in that context,

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:31.440
we're particularly delighted

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.660
to have Barkha join us today,

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.570
who has really on the side of journalism

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:39.420
always sought to provide news,

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:42.450
and information that will help India,

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:44.640
and I think more broadly South Asia,

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:46.443
move towards being more inclusive.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:48.750
Before I introduce Barkha,

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:50.850
let me start by saying that
we couldn't have done it

00:00:50.850 --> 00:00:53.940
without our co-host, the
South Asia Studies Council,

00:00:53.940 --> 00:00:56.340
and the Poynter Fellowship in Journalism.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.860
Many thanks to Kasturi and Amaar at SASC,

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:02.130
and Bliss and Poynter team
for the help planning this.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.350
Also a big thank you to Zahrah, Chris,

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:06.350
and Jenna who helped planned this event.

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:09.330
So let me now briefly introduce Barkha.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.490
I think she probably needs
very little introduction

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:12.420
to the people in the room.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.280
She's one of India's
best known journalists

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:18.060
who has reported for the
last 25 years from war zones,

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:20.550
and conflict areas across the world.

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:23.010
She's also the founding
editor of MoJo Story,

00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:24.750
a multimedia digital platform,

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:26.853
and a columnist for the Washington Post.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:31.170
She's received numerous awards,

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:33.000
but among them are the Padmashri,

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:34.500
and the Global Leader for Tomorrow

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:36.000
from the World Economic Forum.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:38.977
To write her most recent book,

00:01:38.977 --> 00:01:40.337
"To Hell and Back: Humans of COVID,"

00:01:40.337 --> 00:01:44.280
Barkha made multiple pathbreaking
journeys across India

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:47.370
conducting ground reportage
during the pandemic.

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:51.000
And on tonight's event, she's
going to tell us about this.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.650
I should also highlight that today's event

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.890
actually is a flipped
version of a policy dialogue

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:57.870
we had in Delhi in July

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.180
where researchers talked
about their findings

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:01.560
about the pandemic,

00:02:01.560 --> 00:02:05.280
and then Barkha was part
of a discussion on it.

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.440
So today we are going to
continue the conversation

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:08.850
in a flipped version

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:11.580
with Barkha telling us about
her journey through COVID,

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:16.560
and her reflections through
through media in India today.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:18.030
And then after that charity,

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.820
we'll provide some research insights

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:22.920
which complement Barkha's discussion.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.560
Please join me in welcoming Barkha Dutt.

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.560
(audience applauds)

00:02:33.830 --> 00:02:35.596
- Thank you very much.
You can all hear me?

00:02:35.596 --> 00:02:36.492
- [Audience Member] We can.

00:02:36.492 --> 00:02:38.490
- Okay, thank you for coming.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:39.990
Thank you also to all of you

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:44.130
who may be part of this
conversation, but are online,

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:47.010
I hope to have it be an
interactive conversation.

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:49.200
So, looking for lots of questions,

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.460
and comments from the floor,

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:54.033
as well as questions coming
in from our audience online.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:59.580
Okay, what I want to say
before I start talking

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:03.900
about my journey across India
is that reporting on COVID

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.290
really helped me understand many things

00:03:07.290 --> 00:03:09.600
that I did not understand before

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.220
I embarked on this journey.

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:13.620
It helped me understand my country.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.890
It helped me understand
how power is both wielded,

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.200
and organized structurally.

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.430
It helped me understand
my role as a journalist,

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:26.040
and in fact in particular
made me reexamine

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.830
what the place is of media,

00:03:28.830 --> 00:03:32.550
independent media, not just in crisis,

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.980
but even at other times.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.367
So when I wrote this book,

00:03:36.367 --> 00:03:38.910
"To Hell and Back: Humans of COVID,"

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:42.063
above everything else for me,

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:45.120
for all of us who lived
through the pandemic,

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:49.860
we kept hearing things described
in terms of numbers, right?

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:51.060
Numbers are important.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:54.270
Numbers help us actually get a very,

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:56.820
very empirical sense of what's going on.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.520
But we would hear all the time dashboards

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:01.350
that would tell us caseload,

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.770
that would tell us
hospitalizations, mortalities,

00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:06.690
and then later vaccine numbers,

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:08.520
and how many vaccines had been achieved,

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:11.100
and how many booster doses and so on.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:13.290
But who were the people
behind these numbers?

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:15.870
Who were the people who
populated the numbers?

00:04:15.870 --> 00:04:17.640
What were their stories?
What were their names?

00:04:17.640 --> 00:04:20.190
Did we even know their names
and talking about numbers

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:22.680
would all of them even be counted?

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:24.840
I think that was at the heart

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:27.960
of my sort of journey across India.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:29.760
And what I'm going to do
is to give you a sense

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:34.760
of what this in a way taught
me, what I had to unlearn.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:37.560
As some of you might know,

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:40.890
I spent 20 plus years in television

00:04:40.890 --> 00:04:42.540
before I moved to the digital media.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:43.617
Some of you were at lunch today,

00:04:43.617 --> 00:04:47.610
and we were talking about the
incredible shifts in India,

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:49.650
which mirror the shifts globally,

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:53.040
which is just to take you
through some of that data,

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.610
there are 650 million active
internet users in India.

00:04:56.610 --> 00:04:58.710
70% of Indian servants say

00:04:58.710 --> 00:05:03.120
they get their news content
today via the internet.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:06.930
The top two sources of news
are YouTube and WhatsApp.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:08.910
And there are serious questions today

00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:12.510
over broken revenue models
in television newsrooms

00:05:12.510 --> 00:05:14.880
as well as ownership
by big business houses

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.100
that raise questions over how free

00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:20.160
they really are able to
be in their storytelling.

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:23.490
So for a combination of
reasons, shifting technology,

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:24.930
wanting to be my own boss,

00:05:24.930 --> 00:05:28.631
disillusionment with talk TV shops,

00:05:28.631 --> 00:05:31.380
which was what the state
of broadcast media was,

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:34.200
I shifted to forming my
own digital platform.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.440
And as I had registered Mojo
Story, and Mojo means magic,

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:41.280
I set out to look for
the magic of journalism,

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:44.850
which I felt I had forgotten
and I wanted to rediscover.

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:47.340
Two months or three months
later the pandemic hit.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:51.090
So my platform, and my life literally

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:52.170
for the last three years

00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:55.830
has been consumed by the
telling of this story.

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:59.610
So, okay, yeah.

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:03.000
So lemme start with some images

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:08.000
that I think really captured
in a way the unique reality

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:10.500
of how COVID unfolded in India.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:12.570
Unique because every country

00:06:12.570 --> 00:06:14.640
negotiated COVID in its own ways.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:18.090
In every country, COVID
exposed fault lines

00:06:18.090 --> 00:06:20.010
that were unique to that country.

00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:23.010
And in India, we had in the first wave

00:06:23.010 --> 00:06:24.750
something unfold that we hadn't seen

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:27.120
actually since the partition of India,

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:29.730
which was the mass exodus,

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:32.190
and the mass movement of millions

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:35.670
of India's poorest citizens
or daily wage workers.

00:06:35.670 --> 00:06:37.050
Now, why did this happen?

00:06:37.050 --> 00:06:39.570
It happened simply because
like every other country

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:42.120
in the world, India decided to lock down.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:43.590
But when we locked down,

00:06:43.590 --> 00:06:46.740
we also closed public
transport in the first wave.

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:49.620
So in the first wave, you had no buses,

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:54.620
no trains, no airports,
you only had trucks

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:57.060
to some degree plying essential suppliers,

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:00.840
oil, grains and so on, and
that was pretty much it.

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:05.340
And what happened was
that though the government

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:09.090
issued orders saying that
workers, the poor workers,

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:12.580
who live on substance wages must be paid

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:15.570
nobody was paid because economic activity

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:17.250
had come to a grinding halt.

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:19.083
So these were men and women who,

00:07:20.480 --> 00:07:21.570
for those of you who might be familiar

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:23.460
with Indian cities, think
of construction sites

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:25.920
where a house is coming up
or a factory is coming up.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:28.950
They would live on the
site and be given temporary

00:07:28.950 --> 00:07:31.950
like a shack to stay in
while the house was coming up

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:33.900
or they would work as help in the fields.

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:37.560
So they really had no homes
where they had migrated to.

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:40.170
They were just live in these
temporary sort of shelters.

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:41.610
They would get daily wages,

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:43.980
and suddenly they had no source of income.

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:46.470
Secondly, there was an
underestimation of the fact

00:07:46.470 --> 00:07:47.790
that like you and me,

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:50.490
and I think it's easy to
forget this three years later,

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:53.880
but the first wave of COVID
felt like the end of the world.

00:07:53.880 --> 00:07:56.130
You didn't know what
was gonna happen next.

00:07:56.130 --> 00:07:59.460
We ran flights to bring some
of you back home to India,

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:00.570
the Vande Bharat Missions,

00:08:00.570 --> 00:08:04.260
but no transport was actually
open for the poorest workers.

00:08:04.260 --> 00:08:06.480
So they decided men, women,

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.970
and children to start walking home.

00:08:08.970 --> 00:08:10.560
And these were some of the images

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:14.940
that I chronicled as I
followed them across India.

00:08:14.940 --> 00:08:16.800
And what I really found

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:21.800
was that women in particular
had packed their universe's,

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:23.850
their entire universe into a little

00:08:23.850 --> 00:08:26.043
what in Hindi is called a bori, a sack.

00:08:27.066 --> 00:08:28.710
And and they were just,

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:30.120
they didn't know whether
they were gonna come back,

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:31.680
they didn't know if they
were ever going to come back,

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:32.850
they didn't know if they
were going to make it

00:08:32.850 --> 00:08:34.170
to their destinations,

00:08:34.170 --> 00:08:35.460
but their entire universe

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:38.640
packed into a little sack on their head.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:42.390
Sometimes their infants
carried on their shoulders,

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:45.570
often with no food, a packet
of biscuits if they were lucky,

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:47.790
a small bottle of water
if they were lucky.

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:48.840
This is how they walked.

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:50.250
And they walked, as I said,

00:08:50.250 --> 00:08:54.060
hundreds of kilometers often without shoes

00:08:54.060 --> 00:08:55.350
in this coaching heat.

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:58.740
It was still extremely hot in North India

00:08:58.740 --> 00:09:00.300
as they walked home.

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:05.300
To my shock, my (indistinct)
medium television.

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:08.280
And I'm a first generation TV journalist,

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:09.540
I'm a child of television.

00:09:09.540 --> 00:09:12.390
I became a journalist when the airwaves

00:09:12.390 --> 00:09:14.040
were privatized in India.

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:17.880
We only started having
private news in India in 1995.

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:21.423
So it's not that I sort of
was born heating television,

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:25.200
I was literally groomed by the medium,

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:26.640
shaped by the medium.

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:28.740
But to my absolute shock,

00:09:28.740 --> 00:09:33.740
there was hardly any TV presence
with boots on the ground.

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:37.560
Television had decided
to basically look away

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:39.360
from these walking workers,

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:42.930
and concentrate on expert driven talk TV,

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:44.010
which had its place,

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:48.150
but was really not capturing
the scale of what was unfolding

00:09:48.150 --> 00:09:50.550
on sort of the shiny express ways,

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:53.520
which at other moments were
actually an illustration

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:56.250
of how much India's infrastructure story

00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:57.943
had improved and so on.

00:09:57.943 --> 00:10:01.200
And so the story of
these daily wage workers

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:04.290
was told by independent
digital platforms like mine

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:09.210
by regional reporters
in the language press,

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:11.070
by Hindi newspapers,

00:10:11.070 --> 00:10:13.860
and by what in my industry
are called stringers,

00:10:13.860 --> 00:10:15.870
which are really not
professional journalists,

00:10:15.870 --> 00:10:18.090
but let's say you live in a village,

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:19.800
and you have a smartphone,

00:10:19.800 --> 00:10:21.870
you might decide to
take a bunch of pictures

00:10:21.870 --> 00:10:22.703
of what you see,

00:10:22.703 --> 00:10:24.720
and then you'll contribute
that to, let's say,

00:10:24.720 --> 00:10:27.930
a big network headquartered
somewhere in the city.

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:29.820
Television effectively
just didn't get out,

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:32.700
it didn't get out of the metros,
and it sat in the studios.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:37.650
In the meantime, we had
this sort of crisis unfold.

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:39.480
And in some cases,

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:41.330
and this is how I started discovering

00:10:42.330 --> 00:10:44.610
the kind of structural
inequalities is that

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:45.443
at least my work,

00:10:45.443 --> 00:10:47.010
I had been a war correspondent,

00:10:47.010 --> 00:10:48.180
and then a political journalist.

00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:50.640
So I'd done very little of this work,

00:10:50.640 --> 00:10:53.640
and I had never really
looked at the issues

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:57.030
of structural inequalities
through a mainstream media lets.

00:10:57.030 --> 00:10:58.470
And that is what I started doing here.

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:00.420
Now, where I'm sitting right now

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:02.520
is actually the premises
of a major factory

00:11:02.520 --> 00:11:04.350
in India's capital Delhi.

00:11:04.350 --> 00:11:07.170
And this was one of the
more extraordinary things

00:11:07.170 --> 00:11:09.720
I encountered because the
owners of this factory

00:11:09.720 --> 00:11:13.620
as these workers left the cities
to go back to their homes,

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:14.730
and the villages,

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:16.650
they decided that they
must stop the workers

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:18.270
from doing this, right?

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:19.380
And what they did

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:22.650
was they locked up the
workers behind these gates.

00:11:22.650 --> 00:11:24.570
These were the locked
gates as we found them.

00:11:24.570 --> 00:11:26.940
And we somehow managed
to force our way in,

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:30.570
and when we went we
actually found a board,

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:32.910
and the board actually listed the tours,

00:11:32.910 --> 00:11:34.980
and the day that these workers
were allowed to come out

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:37.050
for a cup of tea, and a bathroom break.

00:11:37.050 --> 00:11:39.240
I mean, it was literally
like locking up chattel,

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:41.430
like it was just horrendous.

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:43.200
And they were completely
tone deaf about it.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:44.033
They actually thought

00:11:44.033 --> 00:11:47.340
that they were looking after
the workers by doing this.

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:48.330
And when we walked in,

00:11:48.330 --> 00:11:50.850
we found all of these
desolate, desperate people,

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:52.860
and they were like, "Get
us outta here somehow."

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:55.710
And when we started filming them,

00:11:55.710 --> 00:11:57.930
we had guards come in,
confiscate our tapes,

00:11:57.930 --> 00:11:59.280
try to lock us in.

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:01.530
Of course we made a whole lot of noise.

00:12:01.530 --> 00:12:03.330
I started tweeting the chief minister,

00:12:03.330 --> 00:12:06.570
we managed to get out of
there, but in some ways,

00:12:06.570 --> 00:12:08.490
this was the beginning of my understanding

00:12:08.490 --> 00:12:11.010
that till then there was a media cliche

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:13.560
that COVID was the great equalizer.

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:15.630
But in fact, COVID was the exact opposite

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:16.800
of the great equalizer.

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:20.100
Every structural inequity
of Indian society,

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:21.690
and I'm sure this was true globally,

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:23.160
but I can only speak to India

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:25.350
because that's what I reported on,

00:12:25.350 --> 00:12:27.870
whether it was class, whether it was cast,

00:12:27.870 --> 00:12:32.460
whether it was gender, it was exacerbated,

00:12:32.460 --> 00:12:37.350
it was deepened, that fault
line was just wrenched open,

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:40.650
and you couldn't look away.

00:12:40.650 --> 00:12:43.740
And in fact, I remember meeting in Bombay

00:12:43.740 --> 00:12:46.020
a woman who used to live in the slums,

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:47.940
and she used to work as a domestic help

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:50.610
in the high rise
skyscrapers all around her.

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:52.417
And she said to me, she said,

00:12:52.417 --> 00:12:53.910
"What kind of justice is this?

00:12:53.910 --> 00:12:56.310
I'll say it in Hindi and
then I'll translate it.

00:12:56.310 --> 00:12:57.148
She said,

00:12:57.148 --> 00:13:01.065
(speaking in foreign language)

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:04.023
What kind of justice is this

00:13:04.023 --> 00:13:06.960
that the virus came into India
traveling on an airplane,

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:09.780
and it's the poor person
who's walking on the roads?

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:13.590
Yeah, so I think that's what it was.

00:13:13.590 --> 00:13:15.240
While it impacted everybody,

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:17.250
the way you experienced the pandemic

00:13:17.250 --> 00:13:21.510
very much depended on
what class you were in,

00:13:21.510 --> 00:13:24.480
what gender you were, what
cast you were, and yes,

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:25.980
sometimes what religion you were.

00:13:25.980 --> 00:13:30.600
Because that TV channels did
not put people on the ground,

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:33.780
some of them decided
that this was the moment

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:37.500
to actually start communalizing
the COVID conversation.

00:13:37.500 --> 00:13:38.390
The reason this happened

00:13:38.390 --> 00:13:42.030
is one of the earliest mass
congregations that took place,

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:44.550
that was a super spread of
event, there's no doubt about it,

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:47.880
was by an orthodox Muslim
sect called Tablighi Jamaat.

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:51.330
And this was just at the
cusp of the restrictions

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:52.980
that were then subsequently placed

00:13:52.980 --> 00:13:54.780
on all mass congregations

00:13:54.780 --> 00:13:56.880
except of course election rallies.

00:13:56.880 --> 00:13:58.791
That's a different debate.

00:13:58.791 --> 00:14:02.730
And this undoubtedly was a congregation

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:05.580
that should not have taken
place, but once it did,

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:10.200
we actually had channels using
hashtags like CoronaJihad.

00:14:10.200 --> 00:14:13.170
This was on a major television
channel, The Times Group.

00:14:13.170 --> 00:14:15.810
And there was another network,
the India Today Network,

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:19.530
which represented the COVID dashboard

00:14:19.530 --> 00:14:21.930
graphically in skull caps.

00:14:21.930 --> 00:14:23.520
So they had silhouetted skull caps,

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:28.140
and they put the data
the count of COVID cases

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:30.060
that there was in a skull cap,

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:32.580
and you can get the reference of that.

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:37.580
So one every inequity was
deepened, wrenched open,

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:40.320
therefore for you to see

00:14:40.320 --> 00:14:42.060
unless you were wanting to look away.

00:14:42.060 --> 00:14:47.060
Two you actually had big
media not just look away,

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:50.490
but further sort of add to the toxicity

00:14:50.490 --> 00:14:51.660
around this conversation.

00:14:51.660 --> 00:14:53.450
So there was either an invisibilization

00:14:53.450 --> 00:14:55.080
of what was happening to the poor,

00:14:55.080 --> 00:14:57.420
and in the second we have
a complete invisibilization

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:01.320
of rural India, or there
was a kind of bigoted lens

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:03.320
through which this story was being told.

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:07.290
In the meantime, something very odd,

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:08.760
in India they say that everything you say

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:09.840
in its opposite is true.

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:11.760
And we are a very, very
paradoxical country,

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:13.270
and even on COVID by the way

00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:16.440
one of the big mistakes that India made

00:15:16.440 --> 00:15:18.330
was to order vaccines very late.

00:15:18.330 --> 00:15:20.040
But when we finally got to it

00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:23.580
because we have a history of
mass immunization programs

00:15:23.580 --> 00:15:25.860
it's one of our big
public health successes,

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:28.470
we actually had boats, and drones,

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:31.380
and people walking through
jungles deliver vaccines.

00:15:31.380 --> 00:15:33.180
And we became the second
country in the world

00:15:33.180 --> 00:15:36.610
to cross the 2 billion
mark after China in July

00:15:38.010 --> 00:15:39.210
of the previous year.

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:42.147
So what I'm saying is that
everything you say about India,

00:15:42.147 --> 00:15:44.070
and its opposite is often true.

00:15:44.070 --> 00:15:45.570
There has to be a granularity

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:47.550
in how we understand my country.

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:50.070
And the same thing
happened with the police.

00:15:50.070 --> 00:15:54.420
In this lockdown the police
was first responders,

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:55.710
they were among the first responders.

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:58.590
So they were vulnerable, they were brave.

00:15:58.590 --> 00:16:00.780
I met a policeman whose hand was cut off

00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:03.363
for trying to sort of
enforce the lockdown,

00:16:04.470 --> 00:16:05.490
but on the other hand,

00:16:05.490 --> 00:16:07.470
the police was also weaponized

00:16:07.470 --> 00:16:09.390
sometimes against the poorest citizen.

00:16:09.390 --> 00:16:10.350
So what was happening

00:16:10.350 --> 00:16:12.240
was that these people would try and leave,

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:13.770
the daily wage workers,

00:16:13.770 --> 00:16:17.310
and they would be pushed back
from barricades by the police.

00:16:17.310 --> 00:16:19.890
Some of them would fold
their hands and plead,

00:16:19.890 --> 00:16:21.750
some of them would bribe their way,

00:16:21.750 --> 00:16:23.850
some of them would just revolt and say,

00:16:23.850 --> 00:16:24.840
okay, arrest me if you want to,

00:16:24.840 --> 00:16:26.550
but I'm just breaking this barricade.

00:16:26.550 --> 00:16:28.560
But a lot of people
would just keep walking

00:16:28.560 --> 00:16:30.507
to the city barricade,
and we turn back from it,

00:16:30.507 --> 00:16:35.507
and then have to walk dozens
of sort of miles again

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:38.610
to reach that barricade to be
turned back again and again.

00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:41.880
And this was Seema who was
married to a scrap seller.

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:44.430
He used to just buy what in India

00:16:44.430 --> 00:16:46.620
it's called kabadi, like your waste,

00:16:46.620 --> 00:16:48.150
the newspapers that you finish reading

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:51.570
at the end of the day, tin
cans, basically garbage,

00:16:51.570 --> 00:16:53.610
and repurpose it, right?

00:16:53.610 --> 00:16:55.080
So he was a scrap seller.

00:16:55.080 --> 00:16:56.337
She was married to him,

00:16:56.337 --> 00:16:58.500
and they were trying to
get back to the village,

00:16:58.500 --> 00:17:02.460
and she just collapsed in
tears, literally in my arms,

00:17:02.460 --> 00:17:05.100
making me step back from
my old fashioned notions

00:17:05.100 --> 00:17:07.742
of what is objectivity at this time.

00:17:07.742 --> 00:17:10.500
I'd gone to J school,
and I'd been taught that

00:17:10.500 --> 00:17:12.780
a journalist must have emotional distance

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:13.980
from the story that you're telling,

00:17:13.980 --> 00:17:15.630
but in a situation like this,

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:17.940
how is that emotional distance possible?

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:20.253
Is it even desirable?

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:23.910
Then we come to the story of Punam.

00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:25.830
Now, one of the things that happened,

00:17:25.830 --> 00:17:27.750
and I'm sure Winnie,

00:17:27.750 --> 00:17:31.410
and Charity's data may have
done some work on this is,

00:17:31.410 --> 00:17:34.110
what are the assets that a
poor person has at this time?

00:17:34.110 --> 00:17:34.943
Right?

00:17:34.943 --> 00:17:37.470
So for most of India's poorest people

00:17:37.470 --> 00:17:40.293
the one asset that they owned was a phone.

00:17:41.490 --> 00:17:46.490
So when people didn't know what
to do and how to make ends,

00:17:46.680 --> 00:17:50.190
sort of make ends meet, they
sold the only thing they had,

00:17:50.190 --> 00:17:51.090
which was a phone.

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:55.860
So when I was telling the
story of these migrant workers,

00:17:55.860 --> 00:17:58.350
and I could realize that
there was a class gap

00:17:58.350 --> 00:18:00.090
between my audience, and the story,

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:01.590
and the class of the people I was telling.

00:18:01.590 --> 00:18:03.930
So, now I had an audience

00:18:03.930 --> 00:18:07.443
that was baking banana bread on Instagram,

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:10.980
talking about going to the Maldives,

00:18:10.980 --> 00:18:12.930
talking about the COVID blue of the sky

00:18:12.930 --> 00:18:16.590
that had suddenly been liberated
from Delhi's air pollution.

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:18.570
And I had to make this class of people

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:20.370
care about the poorest worker,

00:18:20.370 --> 00:18:21.930
at a time when, let's face it,

00:18:21.930 --> 00:18:23.970
they were also frightened, right?

00:18:23.970 --> 00:18:26.430
So how were you going to
bridge this cast divide?

00:18:26.430 --> 00:18:28.710
You had to come back to
compelling storytelling.

00:18:28.710 --> 00:18:30.240
So I could have said,

00:18:30.240 --> 00:18:32.100
millions of migrant workers are impacted,

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:33.570
and that would be true, but instead,

00:18:33.570 --> 00:18:37.530
I chose to tell the story of
a man called Mukesh Mandal.

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:39.120
Mukesh Mandal was a house painter,

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:42.210
that's his photograph that
his wife Punam is holding up.

00:18:42.210 --> 00:18:44.220
And he used to do the odd job

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:47.220
painting homes as they came
up on construction sites.

00:18:47.220 --> 00:18:50.010
And like everybody else,
when the lockdown happened

00:18:50.010 --> 00:18:52.020
his work came to a grinding halt,

00:18:52.020 --> 00:18:55.050
and suddenly there was no
food to feed his children.

00:18:55.050 --> 00:18:59.280
So he sold his phone, and he
sold his phone for 2000 rupees.

00:18:59.280 --> 00:19:00.600
With those 2000 rupees,

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:03.570
he bought a table fan for 500
rupees because it was really,

00:19:03.570 --> 00:19:08.160
really hot still in those months of Delhi,

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:10.230
or rather outskirts of
Delhi, he lived in Haryana,

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:14.130
and then he bought some
wheat, he bought some lentils,

00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:16.080
he bought some grains, and
he took it to his wife,

00:19:16.080 --> 00:19:19.860
who was relieved thinking
that at least this week

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:22.830
we won't go hungry, my
children won't go hungry.

00:19:22.830 --> 00:19:26.610
But the next day Mukesh
felt a deep anxiety,

00:19:26.610 --> 00:19:29.520
and he felt that he had
nothing else left to sell.

00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:33.720
He could not see any visible
improvement in the future.

00:19:33.720 --> 00:19:35.580
He didn't know what to do.

00:19:35.580 --> 00:19:36.947
So he went to his wife, Punam,

00:19:36.947 --> 00:19:38.520
and he asked to borrow

00:19:38.520 --> 00:19:41.430
her what we call a dupatta, the scarf.

00:19:41.430 --> 00:19:43.590
And took the dupatta,

00:19:43.590 --> 00:19:46.980
he tied it to the bamboo
pole of the one room element,

00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:48.230
and he took his own life.

00:19:49.080 --> 00:19:53.103
And in my opinion this
was a starvation death.

00:19:54.090 --> 00:19:57.000
It was the fear of hunger
that led him to die.

00:19:57.000 --> 00:19:59.310
He had no visible source of income.

00:19:59.310 --> 00:20:00.510
And in the lockdown

00:20:00.510 --> 00:20:02.490
even the distribution of free Russians,

00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:04.380
which were announced by all governments,

00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:07.650
was extremely difficult
because the lockdown

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:10.680
became like a police enforced curfew

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:15.180
as opposed to a people led
protective measure, right?

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:17.610
So, but the district administration

00:20:17.610 --> 00:20:19.260
that I went to meet subsequently said

00:20:19.260 --> 00:20:20.970
that he was mentally unstable.

00:20:20.970 --> 00:20:22.890
The family was made to
put they were illiterate,

00:20:22.890 --> 00:20:24.900
and they were made to put their thumbprint

00:20:24.900 --> 00:20:28.050
on a set of papers to say that
they didn't even understand

00:20:28.050 --> 00:20:30.000
what they were signing,

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.490
and then the police could
quote that piece of paper,

00:20:32.490 --> 00:20:35.730
and say that he was mentally unstable.

00:20:35.730 --> 00:20:37.740
Meanwhile, the workers kept walking.

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:39.360
You would see images like this

00:20:39.360 --> 00:20:42.037
that they with folded hands
stop a truck driver, say,

00:20:42.037 --> 00:20:43.140
"Please let us get on."

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:44.977
And the truck driver in this case said,

00:20:44.977 --> 00:20:46.860
"No, the police will come after me.

00:20:46.860 --> 00:20:50.070
I can't give you a ride, not
even for a few kilometers."

00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:53.160
And this went on for almost
60 days into the pandemic.

00:20:53.160 --> 00:20:55.680
You had workers walking till this case

00:20:55.680 --> 00:20:57.930
reached the Supreme Court,

00:20:57.930 --> 00:21:00.940
and through forest, through nighttime

00:21:02.310 --> 00:21:06.870
this became a parallel
crisis to the medical crisis

00:21:06.870 --> 00:21:09.690
where already you had beds running short,

00:21:09.690 --> 00:21:11.250
and you didn't have enough doctors.

00:21:11.250 --> 00:21:12.547
And I remember one doctor telling me,

00:21:12.547 --> 00:21:15.930
"I feel like I've been
sent to fight a nuclear war

00:21:15.930 --> 00:21:17.367
with a latte, with a stick."

00:21:19.230 --> 00:21:22.800
One such truck looked
like this at the back.

00:21:22.800 --> 00:21:25.320
Initially, the cops wouldn't
let me see what was happening.

00:21:25.320 --> 00:21:26.760
So I just had to fight with them,

00:21:26.760 --> 00:21:29.370
and insist that I could get a look in.

00:21:29.370 --> 00:21:34.370
And I saw, it looked like
every human trafficking movie

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:35.580
that I had seen.

00:21:35.580 --> 00:21:37.620
It was just like, just these people,

00:21:37.620 --> 00:21:41.070
like lumped together desperate to get out.

00:21:41.070 --> 00:21:42.547
And I asked one of them that,

00:21:42.547 --> 00:21:44.400
"Aren't you scared of getting COVID?"

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:46.470
Like that was a basic question, right?

00:21:46.470 --> 00:21:48.090
Aren't you scared of getting COVID.

00:21:48.090 --> 00:21:50.160
And he said, "Well, the
poverty will kill me

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:51.447
before the virus will."

00:21:52.290 --> 00:21:53.733
And so he left.

00:21:54.660 --> 00:21:57.030
One of the things that occurred to me

00:21:57.030 --> 00:22:02.030
was when I saw these little sort of sacks

00:22:02.190 --> 00:22:04.050
being carried by women
mostly on their head

00:22:04.050 --> 00:22:05.583
as the men carry the children,

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:09.753
what do you carry when you leave,

00:22:11.047 --> 00:22:13.050
and you don't know if you'll
make it to your destination,

00:22:13.050 --> 00:22:14.940
and you don't know
whether you'll come back?

00:22:14.940 --> 00:22:17.370
This was also a question
that partition researchers

00:22:17.370 --> 00:22:18.210
have now looked at.

00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:19.563
What did people carry?

00:22:20.610 --> 00:22:23.940
I come from a kind of refugee family.

00:22:23.940 --> 00:22:25.950
My parents, my father's side of the family

00:22:25.950 --> 00:22:28.050
lived in undivided,

00:22:28.050 --> 00:22:31.470
when India was undivided on
the Pakistan side in Sialkot.

00:22:31.470 --> 00:22:34.230
And I grew up hearing these stories about

00:22:34.230 --> 00:22:36.240
our family had this,
and our family had this,

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:38.100
and there was a piano in the
house, and there was this,

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:40.170
and there was this, and
then we came with nothing.

00:22:40.170 --> 00:22:42.240
Now, of course, many
people came with nothing,

00:22:42.240 --> 00:22:43.590
but even those who came with nothing,

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:45.730
and came as literally poor people

00:22:46.860 --> 00:22:49.680
they went from being wealthy
to being poor overnight.

00:22:49.680 --> 00:22:52.350
Everybody carried something, one thing,

00:22:52.350 --> 00:22:54.300
one thing that meant something to them.

00:22:54.300 --> 00:22:56.460
And so as I saw these people walk,

00:22:56.460 --> 00:22:59.100
I wanted to know what did they carry?

00:22:59.100 --> 00:23:04.100
And the answer was so
moving and humbling for me,

00:23:04.647 --> 00:23:06.180
and this was their answer.

00:23:06.180 --> 00:23:07.740
They carried in a plastic bucket,

00:23:07.740 --> 00:23:10.240
so many of them that I met
their workman students.

00:23:11.670 --> 00:23:13.590
And, of course, they
carried a couple of clothes,

00:23:13.590 --> 00:23:15.060
and maybe a packet of biscuits,

00:23:15.060 --> 00:23:16.620
and a blanket for their child,

00:23:16.620 --> 00:23:18.150
but they carried their what in Hindi

00:23:18.150 --> 00:23:20.250
is called their ozar, their tools.

00:23:20.250 --> 00:23:22.860
And they said, "Maybe
we'll get work on the way.

00:23:22.860 --> 00:23:24.720
Maybe something, maybe
there'll be somebody

00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:26.310
who will give us work."

00:23:26.310 --> 00:23:29.550
So even through this calamity,
even through this crisis,

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:30.780
these were people who were desperate.

00:23:30.780 --> 00:23:31.740
They were not begging,

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:33.720
they were not saying, give us a dough.

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:36.720
They were just like, "We wanna
work, but there is no work."

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:38.730
And at that point

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:40.680
there was no sort of social security net

00:23:40.680 --> 00:23:42.900
because they were like a displaced,

00:23:42.900 --> 00:23:45.840
they were at that point an
internally displaced community.

00:23:45.840 --> 00:23:49.320
So the idea that they
could get to a bank account

00:23:49.320 --> 00:23:52.140
where a direct cash
transfer had taken place,

00:23:52.140 --> 00:23:56.130
or through this sort of barricaded police

00:23:56.130 --> 00:23:59.160
monitoring of movement,

00:23:59.160 --> 00:24:02.220
they would even be able to
access a social welfare program

00:24:02.220 --> 00:24:04.830
was an unrealistic sort of idea.

00:24:04.830 --> 00:24:06.270
And so they carried their tools.

00:24:06.270 --> 00:24:09.270
This was somewhere in
Telangana in the south,

00:24:09.270 --> 00:24:11.100
and then there was this
nine year old child.

00:24:11.100 --> 00:24:13.477
And I playfully asked him, I said,

00:24:13.477 --> 00:24:15.540
"Oh, do you know what coronavirus is?"

00:24:15.540 --> 00:24:18.690
By this time some public
transport had started

00:24:18.690 --> 00:24:20.670
with the intervention
of the Supreme Court,

00:24:20.670 --> 00:24:21.990
and he was standing in line

00:24:21.990 --> 00:24:25.230
to take a bus back with
his father to his village.

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:26.670
And he said, "Of course
I know what it means."

00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:27.660
And I said, "What does it mean?"

00:24:27.660 --> 00:24:31.050
And he said, "It means I
don't get food to eat."

00:24:31.050 --> 00:24:33.810
And that was a nine year
old child's understanding.

00:24:33.810 --> 00:24:35.967
So, when we look at COVID,

00:24:35.967 --> 00:24:39.631
and the pandemic as a medical
story, as a story of science,

00:24:39.631 --> 00:24:44.631
as a story of doctors and drugs,
it is all of those things.

00:24:45.150 --> 00:24:47.070
But for so many countries

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:49.920
it is also a story of power,

00:24:49.920 --> 00:24:53.610
and access to resources and inequity.

00:24:53.610 --> 00:24:56.490
But it's also a story
of hope, and compassion,

00:24:56.490 --> 00:25:00.900
and kindness as I subsequently
discovered in my story,

00:25:00.900 --> 00:25:04.410
in my journey, one of the odd
things that happened in India

00:25:04.410 --> 00:25:08.160
was that there was either
too much centralization

00:25:08.160 --> 00:25:09.930
or too little centralization.

00:25:09.930 --> 00:25:12.420
So sometimes you just
had entire districts,

00:25:12.420 --> 00:25:14.220
and it was left to the
district magistrate,

00:25:14.220 --> 00:25:17.220
the local bureaucrat in the
area to take a decision.

00:25:17.220 --> 00:25:19.860
And at other times, like
with the use of police

00:25:19.860 --> 00:25:22.530
you had too much state or to little state.

00:25:22.530 --> 00:25:25.860
The overarching presence of the police

00:25:25.860 --> 00:25:27.630
was the state when you didn't need it.

00:25:27.630 --> 00:25:29.700
And outside the hospitals,

00:25:29.700 --> 00:25:31.890
when people would go from
hospital to hospital,

00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:34.890
and there was not one person
either at the state level

00:25:34.890 --> 00:25:37.230
or at the central level to
tell them where to go next.

00:25:37.230 --> 00:25:40.020
And I witnessed people dying on the road

00:25:40.020 --> 00:25:43.530
at the closed doors of hospital gates.

00:25:43.530 --> 00:25:44.640
There was no state.

00:25:44.640 --> 00:25:47.550
So when you needed the
state it went missing,

00:25:47.550 --> 00:25:50.940
when you didn't want the state
there was too much of it.

00:25:50.940 --> 00:25:55.440
Old people suffered in an,
I'm sure for those of you

00:25:55.440 --> 00:25:57.450
who were here during the pandemic,

00:25:57.450 --> 00:25:59.100
were any of you here during the pandemic

00:25:59.100 --> 00:26:00.930
with families back home?

00:26:00.930 --> 00:26:02.970
With families back home?

00:26:02.970 --> 00:26:06.090
Okay, so you know what you
would've been going through

00:26:06.090 --> 00:26:08.890
thinking about your parents
or your grandparents, right?

00:26:10.320 --> 00:26:13.320
It's really strange, but
of all the stories I did

00:26:13.320 --> 00:26:18.320
this one just cut across
classes and genres of viewers,

00:26:18.810 --> 00:26:22.500
because I think it spoke
either to everybody's concern

00:26:22.500 --> 00:26:23.640
about their parents

00:26:23.640 --> 00:26:26.010
or it spoke to everybody's
guilt about their parents,

00:26:26.010 --> 00:26:28.863
or it spoke to everybody's
fear about growing old.

00:26:29.730 --> 00:26:33.660
And this was Lilavat,
who was 70 years old,

00:26:33.660 --> 00:26:35.130
and she was a widow.

00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:37.350
She had come to meet her son in Bombay,

00:26:37.350 --> 00:26:39.030
and then when the lockdown hit,

00:26:39.030 --> 00:26:40.620
the son decided that he could not look

00:26:40.620 --> 00:26:42.030
after his aging mother.

00:26:42.030 --> 00:26:43.740
He didn't have any source of income,

00:26:43.740 --> 00:26:46.260
and he was an alcoholic,
so he threw her out.

00:26:46.260 --> 00:26:49.770
And because I was now
following the workers,

00:26:49.770 --> 00:26:51.360
and by now trains had started running,

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:54.180
I met her sitting outside
railway station in Bombay,

00:26:54.180 --> 00:26:57.450
and she'd had no food, no money.

00:26:57.450 --> 00:26:58.890
And I said, "Where are you going?"

00:26:58.890 --> 00:27:00.780
She said, "I don't know,
can you put me on a train?"

00:27:00.780 --> 00:27:03.180
And I said, "But even
if I put you on a train,

00:27:03.180 --> 00:27:04.710
where should I send you?"

00:27:04.710 --> 00:27:07.350
And she said, "None of
my children will have me,

00:27:07.350 --> 00:27:08.850
but can you send me to Delhi?

00:27:08.850 --> 00:27:10.560
Because that's the city I know best

00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:13.920
because I used to live there
when my husband was alive."

00:27:13.920 --> 00:27:14.753
So I said, "But okay,

00:27:14.753 --> 00:27:16.260
I'll send you to Delhi,
but what will you do?"

00:27:16.260 --> 00:27:18.390
And she said, "I'll beg."

00:27:18.390 --> 00:27:20.703
And I didn't know what to do.

00:27:21.690 --> 00:27:23.550
All I could do was to tell her story,

00:27:23.550 --> 00:27:25.350
and hope that somebody would take notice.

00:27:25.350 --> 00:27:27.840
And that is what happened,

00:27:27.840 --> 00:27:30.000
which is that when we released the story,

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:32.580
the railways then took
her into the waiting room,

00:27:32.580 --> 00:27:33.720
They put her on a train,

00:27:33.720 --> 00:27:37.350
and a politician in Delhi
actually took her in

00:27:37.350 --> 00:27:40.650
as a temporary measure till her daughter

00:27:40.650 --> 00:27:42.240
agreed to take her back.

00:27:42.240 --> 00:27:43.650
But the story of Lilavat,

00:27:43.650 --> 00:27:45.150
I mean, I have never seen,

00:27:45.150 --> 00:27:46.740
like until this date three years later

00:27:46.740 --> 00:27:48.270
somebody finds a story on Mojo Story.

00:27:48.270 --> 00:27:50.340
They write to me saying, "Can
we financially help her?"

00:27:50.340 --> 00:27:52.260
And it spoke to all of our,

00:27:52.260 --> 00:27:55.230
I think, collective vulnerability.

00:27:55.230 --> 00:27:56.580
This was Jyoti.

00:27:56.580 --> 00:27:58.740
Jyoti got the attention of Ivanka Trump

00:27:58.740 --> 00:28:00.330
for all the wrong reasons,

00:28:00.330 --> 00:28:03.210
who decided to treat her trauma
as a sporting achievement

00:28:03.210 --> 00:28:04.650
instead of the trauma that it was.

00:28:04.650 --> 00:28:08.010
She was a 15 year old who
cycled with her father

00:28:08.010 --> 00:28:09.120
at the back of the cycle,

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:11.820
1200 kilometers from Delhi to Bihar.

00:28:11.820 --> 00:28:14.760
The reason that the father
couldn't ride the cycle

00:28:14.760 --> 00:28:17.160
is because he just had a knee
surgery, so he couldn't pedal.

00:28:17.160 --> 00:28:19.320
The cycle was luxury most
other people were walking,

00:28:19.320 --> 00:28:23.040
and those shoes feted by the
world as this sort of champion.

00:28:23.040 --> 00:28:24.799
And she was given many free cycles,

00:28:24.799 --> 00:28:27.663
like what was she gonna
do with those cycles?

00:28:28.950 --> 00:28:31.050
She kept saying, and I met her twice,

00:28:31.050 --> 00:28:33.840
and she kept saying all
I want to do is study.

00:28:33.840 --> 00:28:36.180
And that is literally my only dream,

00:28:36.180 --> 00:28:37.500
and I cried every night.

00:28:37.500 --> 00:28:39.000
We would stay at the gas stations,

00:28:39.000 --> 00:28:40.230
the petrol pumps on the highway.

00:28:40.230 --> 00:28:41.550
I was a young girl.

00:28:41.550 --> 00:28:42.383
That was a family later,

00:28:42.383 --> 00:28:44.730
is when the few happy
endings of this story.

00:28:44.730 --> 00:28:48.000
she became such a global star

00:28:48.000 --> 00:28:51.840
that her house became the first
brick house in her village

00:28:51.840 --> 00:28:55.980
because people did offer
to financially support her.

00:28:55.980 --> 00:28:57.840
There's now a movie being made on her.

00:28:57.840 --> 00:28:59.310
But again, I will say

00:28:59.310 --> 00:29:01.410
that there was a kind of
romancing of this story,

00:29:01.410 --> 00:29:04.012
a romanticization of the story.

00:29:04.012 --> 00:29:06.780
No 15 year old girls should
have to cycle back home

00:29:06.780 --> 00:29:08.340
through a pandemic with her father

00:29:08.340 --> 00:29:10.830
at the back of the cycle
through a lockdown.

00:29:10.830 --> 00:29:12.390
She should never have had to do that.

00:29:12.390 --> 00:29:14.463
And there was a great romanticism.

00:29:15.360 --> 00:29:17.557
I met her in her village
in Bihar, and she said,

00:29:17.557 --> 00:29:19.680
"I've got 15 cycles, would
you like to try one?"

00:29:19.680 --> 00:29:20.940
So we went to a cycle, right?

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:22.795
She took me to her school and she said,

00:29:22.795 --> 00:29:24.300
"All I want to do is study."

00:29:24.300 --> 00:29:26.430
One of the other things
that we don't talk about,

00:29:26.430 --> 00:29:29.070
and I really felt strongly
about this during the pandemic

00:29:29.070 --> 00:29:31.370
is what happened to other
people who were ill?

00:29:32.880 --> 00:29:35.790
Other illnesses did not stop.

00:29:35.790 --> 00:29:37.800
And so whether it was kidney disease,

00:29:37.800 --> 00:29:38.940
whether it was cancer,

00:29:38.940 --> 00:29:40.890
whether it was little
children falling ill,

00:29:40.890 --> 00:29:43.680
needing a heart surgery,
we saw this happening.

00:29:43.680 --> 00:29:45.690
We saw poor people in particular

00:29:45.690 --> 00:29:48.960
come to the sort of gates
of public health hospitals

00:29:48.960 --> 00:29:50.550
because that's all they could afford.

00:29:50.550 --> 00:29:52.230
Those hospitals were taken over,

00:29:52.230 --> 00:29:54.300
and become totally COVID hospitals.

00:29:54.300 --> 00:29:58.803
And so suddenly you had
people bearing their children.

00:29:59.700 --> 00:30:02.816
These were five girls whose
father died from tuberculosis.

00:30:02.816 --> 00:30:05.130
He was a tea vendor.

00:30:05.130 --> 00:30:06.360
This was a cancer patient

00:30:06.360 --> 00:30:08.070
I met outside one of the hospitals,

00:30:08.070 --> 00:30:10.057
and he was just told by the guard,

00:30:10.057 --> 00:30:11.267
"Now you go to the next hospital."

00:30:11.267 --> 00:30:13.470
He was like, "What's the next hospital?"

00:30:13.470 --> 00:30:16.320
And no one could tell him where to go.

00:30:16.320 --> 00:30:18.150
So he was just this lonely man

00:30:18.150 --> 00:30:19.380
I just met outside

00:30:19.380 --> 00:30:21.510
one of the public health
hospitals in Delhi,

00:30:21.510 --> 00:30:23.520
and he was just walking.

00:30:23.520 --> 00:30:24.750
Those are the five girls

00:30:24.750 --> 00:30:26.880
who lost their father to tuberculosis.

00:30:26.880 --> 00:30:27.713
Now, this story,

00:30:27.713 --> 00:30:29.430
I was talking about the
paradox of the police

00:30:29.430 --> 00:30:32.970
being both vulnerable, brave frontliners,

00:30:32.970 --> 00:30:36.780
and also weaponized against
the most vulnerable citizens.

00:30:36.780 --> 00:30:37.680
This was Manoj.

00:30:37.680 --> 00:30:42.240
He used to ride rickshaw,
an electronic richshaw.

00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:44.490
And because there was no food,

00:30:44.490 --> 00:30:46.530
he decided that he wanted to go,

00:30:46.530 --> 00:30:49.320
and buy some vegetables
from the wholesale market.

00:30:49.320 --> 00:30:51.810
So he wandered outside
of the permissible area

00:30:51.810 --> 00:30:53.820
that was allowed in the lockdown,

00:30:53.820 --> 00:30:55.650
and the police got hold of him,

00:30:55.650 --> 00:30:59.070
they beat him, and his arm was fractured.

00:30:59.070 --> 00:31:01.710
They beat him, and they
deposited him outside a hospital,

00:31:01.710 --> 00:31:03.480
which wouldn't take him in.

00:31:03.480 --> 00:31:07.620
And because truth is so much
more dramatic than fiction,

00:31:07.620 --> 00:31:10.530
this image to me was the most ironic image

00:31:10.530 --> 00:31:14.580
because I found him leaning
against this police barricade,

00:31:14.580 --> 00:31:15.990
beaten by the police,

00:31:15.990 --> 00:31:18.540
left by the police outside this hospital,

00:31:18.540 --> 00:31:19.893
which wouldn't take him in.

00:31:20.970 --> 00:31:24.630
And what did he do? He just
went out to buy vegetables.

00:31:24.630 --> 00:31:27.360
These were workers who
died in a road accident.

00:31:27.360 --> 00:31:29.760
Remember they were taking
lifts where they could,

00:31:29.760 --> 00:31:32.820
and their bodies were
shoved into plastic packets,

00:31:32.820 --> 00:31:34.740
and sent back home on ice slabs,

00:31:34.740 --> 00:31:38.160
the only surviving worker of this accident

00:31:38.160 --> 00:31:42.780
said that we are being
treated worse than insects.

00:31:42.780 --> 00:31:45.210
Those are scenes from that accident side.

00:31:45.210 --> 00:31:49.380
And this by the time trains were opening,

00:31:49.380 --> 00:31:50.910
it started with freight trains,

00:31:50.910 --> 00:31:53.410
good trains that were
carrying essential supplies.

00:31:55.410 --> 00:31:57.930
Tribal workers decided,

00:31:57.930 --> 00:31:59.940
they came from a very, very remote village

00:31:59.940 --> 00:32:02.100
in the heartland of
India in Madhya Pradesh,

00:32:02.100 --> 00:32:04.890
and according to me
sometimes a single image

00:32:04.890 --> 00:32:06.480
tells you the whole story of a pandemic.

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:08.777
And the image I'm going
to show you next to me

00:32:08.777 --> 00:32:11.880
is I think probably
the most stirring image

00:32:11.880 --> 00:32:13.480
I have seen out of the pandemic.

00:32:14.550 --> 00:32:17.103
These were men who, and they were all men,

00:32:18.270 --> 00:32:20.100
they were walking home
hundreds of kilometers

00:32:20.100 --> 00:32:21.060
like everybody else,

00:32:21.060 --> 00:32:22.920
and they decided to sleep

00:32:22.920 --> 00:32:25.320
on the rail tracks for two reasons.

00:32:25.320 --> 00:32:27.300
They thought there are no passenger trains

00:32:27.300 --> 00:32:29.070
because there were no passenger trains.

00:32:29.070 --> 00:32:30.900
And two, they thought they
could hide from the police

00:32:30.900 --> 00:32:32.430
who might send them back,

00:32:32.430 --> 00:32:34.560
but as they laid there
at 4:00 in the morning,

00:32:34.560 --> 00:32:37.020
a good strain that was
carrying supplies came,

00:32:37.020 --> 00:32:38.430
and ran over them.

00:32:38.430 --> 00:32:40.080
All 18 of them died.

00:32:40.080 --> 00:32:42.300
And this is all that remained of them.

00:32:42.300 --> 00:32:44.900
A little packet of roti, a
bread that they'd packed.

00:32:45.750 --> 00:32:48.270
Like I said food was scarce.

00:32:48.270 --> 00:32:50.370
Even if you had money there was no shops,

00:32:50.370 --> 00:32:55.370
no harbors, no roadside sort
of properties that were open,

00:32:56.610 --> 00:33:00.140
and this was what remained of them.

00:33:00.140 --> 00:33:02.490
And I went to their village.

00:33:02.490 --> 00:33:05.100
It was literally on my
100 day on the road,

00:33:05.100 --> 00:33:05.933
I went to their village,

00:33:05.933 --> 00:33:08.760
and it was the toughest place to get to.

00:33:08.760 --> 00:33:11.190
I said, "This is someone
who has covered war,

00:33:11.190 --> 00:33:13.800
and riots and calamity."

00:33:13.800 --> 00:33:15.450
That it was such a remote village

00:33:15.450 --> 00:33:18.060
that it took me 18 hours by road,

00:33:18.060 --> 00:33:20.290
and then another few hours on foot

00:33:21.480 --> 00:33:23.250
to get to where they came from.

00:33:23.250 --> 00:33:26.700
And nobody had thought,
who were these people

00:33:26.700 --> 00:33:27.960
who died on the rail tracks?

00:33:27.960 --> 00:33:29.940
What was the name of the village?

00:33:29.940 --> 00:33:31.500
What were their names?

00:33:31.500 --> 00:33:33.450
And I met their families.

00:33:33.450 --> 00:33:37.200
And like this, we moved
into the second wave.

00:33:37.200 --> 00:33:40.020
And in the second wave we learned
a lot from the first wave,

00:33:40.020 --> 00:33:42.210
we did not shut public transport.

00:33:42.210 --> 00:33:45.870
We learned that hospitals
should not be converted

00:33:45.870 --> 00:33:48.210
into COVID only hospitals
because there are people dying

00:33:48.210 --> 00:33:51.510
from other ailments that also
need medical intervention.

00:33:51.510 --> 00:33:55.260
But this was a pregnant, a new mother,

00:33:55.260 --> 00:33:56.093
I beg your pardon,

00:33:56.093 --> 00:33:59.580
who just gave birth to a one day old baby.

00:33:59.580 --> 00:34:01.650
And she had COVID, she
had no hospital to go to.

00:34:01.650 --> 00:34:03.510
Now this became the crisis, right?

00:34:03.510 --> 00:34:06.390
Now you couldn't get a bed in hospital,

00:34:06.390 --> 00:34:08.520
you just couldn't get a bed in hospital.

00:34:08.520 --> 00:34:10.800
And if you could get a bed in hospital,

00:34:10.800 --> 00:34:12.510
you couldn't get oxygen.

00:34:12.510 --> 00:34:15.450
And I met people who were made to sign

00:34:15.450 --> 00:34:17.700
what I have called
modern day death warrants

00:34:17.700 --> 00:34:20.520
because they were just made
to sign waivers saying,

00:34:20.520 --> 00:34:23.550
and it is in the
hospital's fault, frankly,

00:34:23.550 --> 00:34:25.530
because they didn't have the oxygen.

00:34:25.530 --> 00:34:27.360
They were made to sign warrants saying

00:34:27.360 --> 00:34:30.180
that if your loved one dies
from a shortage of oxygen,

00:34:30.180 --> 00:34:32.823
you will not sue the hospital, right?

00:34:33.750 --> 00:34:35.940
I saw something I never
thought I would see in my life.

00:34:35.940 --> 00:34:38.430
Gurdwaras which are
Sikh places of worship

00:34:38.430 --> 00:34:40.170
normally have a what's called a langar,

00:34:40.170 --> 00:34:44.388
which is a free meal for
the very poor, right?

00:34:44.388 --> 00:34:45.221
(speaking in foreign language)

00:34:45.221 --> 00:34:47.430
I mean, some of you would
be familiar with the langar.

00:34:47.430 --> 00:34:50.790
Gurdwaras in India
started oxygen langars.

00:34:50.790 --> 00:34:53.580
Now, this is also about the hope,

00:34:53.580 --> 00:34:55.350
and the compassion that one saw

00:34:55.350 --> 00:34:57.800
where governments were
missing people stepped in.

00:34:58.860 --> 00:35:02.640
Social media for once
became a mechanism for good.

00:35:02.640 --> 00:35:06.510
People would say, do you know
someone who can supply oxygen?

00:35:06.510 --> 00:35:09.000
Do you know someone who can
give me so and so supply,

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:12.090
and Gurudwaras started what
was called oxygen langars.

00:35:12.090 --> 00:35:13.530
And you would see scenes like this

00:35:13.530 --> 00:35:15.330
where there would be
giant oxygen cylinders

00:35:15.330 --> 00:35:16.650
placed on the road.

00:35:16.650 --> 00:35:19.410
Somebody could drive in
or come on a rickshaw,

00:35:19.410 --> 00:35:20.970
poor, rich, it didn't matter.

00:35:20.970 --> 00:35:24.180
You were allowed to have access
to a single oxygen cylinder

00:35:24.180 --> 00:35:26.190
for half an hour then you had to move on,

00:35:26.190 --> 00:35:28.020
and make room for the next person.

00:35:28.020 --> 00:35:30.500
It was like the McDonald's of oxygen.

00:35:30.500 --> 00:35:32.100
It was like a drive through.

00:35:32.100 --> 00:35:35.310
And it feels almost like weird now

00:35:35.310 --> 00:35:37.680
for me to say it like this,
but this is what it was.

00:35:37.680 --> 00:35:40.890
It was a drive through oxygen langar.

00:35:40.890 --> 00:35:43.650
In the meantime, people started
taking cardboard strips.

00:35:43.650 --> 00:35:46.260
I actually met a family that
slept on cardboard strips

00:35:46.260 --> 00:35:49.170
on the floor of this
was a Bombay Hospital.

00:35:49.170 --> 00:35:52.290
These were the images from
our cremation grounds,

00:35:52.290 --> 00:35:56.310
our graveyards, our hospitals,
just absolute devastation.

00:35:56.310 --> 00:36:00.000
And now we come to this image
which became a major, major,

00:36:00.000 --> 00:36:04.320
major fault line in India's
internal discussions.

00:36:04.320 --> 00:36:06.210
How many people have died from COVID?

00:36:06.210 --> 00:36:07.980
We started this conversation

00:36:07.980 --> 00:36:10.380
by saying that numbers
didn't tell the full story.

00:36:10.380 --> 00:36:12.810
You needed to know the
people behind the numbers.

00:36:12.810 --> 00:36:15.060
The unfortunate dimension to this

00:36:15.060 --> 00:36:17.310
is we don't even know the numbers, right?

00:36:17.310 --> 00:36:21.840
The epidemiologists all
vary in their estimation.

00:36:21.840 --> 00:36:24.990
The World Health Organization
says 10 times more people

00:36:24.990 --> 00:36:27.140
have died in India than
the official count.

00:36:28.800 --> 00:36:32.040
In the middle of all of this,
I remember this day well,

00:36:32.040 --> 00:36:33.840
it was one day before my father died.

00:36:33.840 --> 00:36:38.534
My father died from COVID, and
it was very strange for me.

00:36:38.534 --> 00:36:40.440
We were in the second wave by now.

00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:42.120
This had been my whole life,

00:36:42.120 --> 00:36:45.990
and I literally became the
story I had been reporting.

00:36:45.990 --> 00:36:48.480
Obviously, [36:47] with all the caveats

00:36:48.480 --> 00:36:49.740
that I came from privilege.

00:36:49.740 --> 00:36:51.420
When my father couldn't
get a hospital bed,

00:36:51.420 --> 00:36:52.710
I was able to call a doctor.

00:36:52.710 --> 00:36:55.980
I was able to say, "Please
get him a hospital bed."

00:36:55.980 --> 00:36:59.520
The doctor agreed, but the
hospital was an hour away.

00:36:59.520 --> 00:37:04.300
And there was no ambulance
available at that time.

00:37:04.300 --> 00:37:06.810
And I made a mistake that
continues to haunt me,

00:37:06.810 --> 00:37:08.310
that we, my sister and I,

00:37:08.310 --> 00:37:10.140
got what was called a private agency.

00:37:10.140 --> 00:37:12.330
And they said, it's a private ambulance,

00:37:12.330 --> 00:37:14.820
but when the so-called ambulance arrived

00:37:14.820 --> 00:37:18.450
it was a broken down van, like
a really old ramshackled van.

00:37:18.450 --> 00:37:22.470
It had a hard bench at the
back, it had a kind of rusty,

00:37:22.470 --> 00:37:24.750
giant oxygen cylinder lying on the floor.

00:37:24.750 --> 00:37:27.030
And he didn't even have a mask.

00:37:27.030 --> 00:37:28.710
There was only a driver,
there were no paramedics.

00:37:28.710 --> 00:37:30.240
And I had to make a split second decision,

00:37:30.240 --> 00:37:31.590
do I send this guy away?

00:37:31.590 --> 00:37:34.020
Do I just wait for the hospital ambulance?

00:37:34.020 --> 00:37:35.940
And I made the decision
to jump on the front seat,

00:37:35.940 --> 00:37:38.850
and go with my father
all the while driving

00:37:38.850 --> 00:37:40.170
through a snarl of traffic

00:37:40.170 --> 00:37:42.240
that had again been created
by police barricades

00:37:42.240 --> 00:37:44.310
who were trying to enforce a lockdown.

00:37:44.310 --> 00:37:46.800
Anyway, there was a whole
mile long of other daughters

00:37:46.800 --> 00:37:48.990
possibly in ambulances with their fathers,

00:37:48.990 --> 00:37:51.210
and here we go to this hospital.

00:37:51.210 --> 00:37:53.160
And by the time we reached the hospital

00:37:56.370 --> 00:37:58.680
my father was supposed to be
admitted to a general room,

00:37:58.680 --> 00:38:01.947
and the doctors on emergency
duty, they checked his oxygen,

00:38:01.947 --> 00:38:04.470
and they found that the
cylinder hadn't worked

00:38:04.470 --> 00:38:05.670
as it was meant to.

00:38:05.670 --> 00:38:07.560
So, his oxygen levels had plummeted,

00:38:07.560 --> 00:38:09.090
and he had to be admitted to an ICU.

00:38:09.090 --> 00:38:10.260
There was no ICU bed.

00:38:10.260 --> 00:38:12.120
And literally I had spent a year

00:38:12.120 --> 00:38:14.130
and a half telling this
story about other people,

00:38:14.130 --> 00:38:15.750
people outside hospitals,

00:38:15.750 --> 00:38:18.360
people not able to get
a bed, people desperate.

00:38:18.360 --> 00:38:20.010
I now became that desperate person.

00:38:20.010 --> 00:38:21.840
Please, I need an ICU bed.

00:38:21.840 --> 00:38:23.160
We got the ICU bed,

00:38:23.160 --> 00:38:26.070
but it was the last time
that I saw him alive.

00:38:26.070 --> 00:38:28.710
And five days later when he died,

00:38:28.710 --> 00:38:32.020
we had to cremate him near the hospital

00:38:33.240 --> 00:38:36.540
because the hospital was
technically in another state,

00:38:36.540 --> 00:38:38.040
and it would've needed police permission

00:38:38.040 --> 00:38:38.880
to bring him back home.

00:38:38.880 --> 00:38:40.630
So we couldn't bring him back home.

00:38:41.760 --> 00:38:43.200
And at the cremation ground

00:38:43.200 --> 00:38:44.580
there was no space to cremate him.

00:38:44.580 --> 00:38:47.320
So we actually had to
call the police to say

00:38:48.240 --> 00:38:50.400
that can you please help
us cremate our father?

00:38:50.400 --> 00:38:54.540
And that evening I tested
positive for COVID.

00:38:54.540 --> 00:38:58.140
So it was very surreal,
but I remember this image

00:38:58.140 --> 00:38:59.130
because it was the evening

00:38:59.130 --> 00:39:01.590
before I took my father to hospital,

00:39:01.590 --> 00:39:04.923
and it was a cremation ground
where this was the image.

00:39:07.554 --> 00:39:10.350
And the caretaker of the
cremation ground told me

00:39:10.350 --> 00:39:12.420
that the official number from the city

00:39:12.420 --> 00:39:17.190
that week had been 30, but he'd
handled 300 bodies in a day.

00:39:17.190 --> 00:39:20.460
And so it became my kind of
mission after I got better.

00:39:20.460 --> 00:39:21.390
It was also the only way

00:39:21.390 --> 00:39:23.610
I knew how to deal with loss and grief,

00:39:23.610 --> 00:39:25.500
that I was going to change the story

00:39:25.500 --> 00:39:29.400
of the uncounted COVID deaths,
because I felt that, I mean,

00:39:29.400 --> 00:39:31.770
we had criticized the media ourselves

00:39:31.770 --> 00:39:33.240
saying people can't just be numbers.

00:39:33.240 --> 00:39:35.340
And now we were saying
they weren't even numbers,

00:39:35.340 --> 00:39:38.940
but when we had these
images, and some of us did,

00:39:38.940 --> 00:39:42.420
we were called vultures by trolls

00:39:42.420 --> 00:39:44.370
who said that we were
feasting off the dead,

00:39:44.370 --> 00:39:47.700
and we were sort of why were
we showing these images?

00:39:47.700 --> 00:39:49.500
And look at the American press

00:39:49.500 --> 00:39:51.570
because it never does
a story on mass graves.

00:39:51.570 --> 00:39:52.403
This is not true.

00:39:52.403 --> 00:39:54.120
I personally pulled out several stories

00:39:54.120 --> 00:39:56.460
from the front pages of the
post to The New York Times

00:39:56.460 --> 00:39:58.590
that had actually done similar stories

00:39:58.590 --> 00:40:00.450
on uncounted COVID deaths

00:40:00.450 --> 00:40:01.980
here in the United States of America.

00:40:01.980 --> 00:40:04.113
So it's not that this did not happen here,

00:40:05.070 --> 00:40:08.160
but then this was another cremation ground

00:40:08.160 --> 00:40:10.170
where literally bodies were
lying on the floor like this

00:40:10.170 --> 00:40:15.150
because there was just no
place to actually cremate them.

00:40:15.150 --> 00:40:18.120
This is drone footage of
the banks of the Ganga.

00:40:18.120 --> 00:40:20.010
So before I show you the next few visuals,

00:40:20.010 --> 00:40:21.960
I just want you to understand
what was happening.

00:40:21.960 --> 00:40:23.250
Remember we spoke in the beginning

00:40:23.250 --> 00:40:25.920
of invisibilization by the media,

00:40:25.920 --> 00:40:27.930
of a number of things that were happening.

00:40:27.930 --> 00:40:30.060
By the time the second wave happened,

00:40:30.060 --> 00:40:31.230
if in the first wave

00:40:31.230 --> 00:40:33.330
the daily wage workers
had been invisibilized

00:40:33.330 --> 00:40:34.590
by the second wave,

00:40:34.590 --> 00:40:37.230
rural India did not
figure in COVID reporting,

00:40:37.230 --> 00:40:39.270
it just didn't figure in COVID reporting.

00:40:39.270 --> 00:40:41.280
Now, what was happening
in the villages of India

00:40:41.280 --> 00:40:44.370
is that people were not testing,

00:40:44.370 --> 00:40:47.160
either they were not testing
because there were stigma

00:40:47.160 --> 00:40:47.993
or they were not testing

00:40:47.993 --> 00:40:50.010
because there was no access to testing,

00:40:50.010 --> 00:40:51.450
or if they were testing

00:40:51.450 --> 00:40:52.380
they didn't know what to do next

00:40:52.380 --> 00:40:55.713
because hospitals weren't
close by enough to access.

00:40:56.580 --> 00:40:58.860
So what started happening is that

00:40:58.860 --> 00:41:00.420
if you would go to villages,

00:41:00.420 --> 00:41:02.370
you would find that the
Panchayat would tell you

00:41:02.370 --> 00:41:06.570
the local sort of village
governance module would tell you

00:41:06.570 --> 00:41:09.450
that in these last two months we have seen

00:41:09.450 --> 00:41:13.378
a surge in the number of
people dying from fever, and

00:41:13.378 --> 00:41:14.310
(speaks in foreign language)

00:41:14.310 --> 00:41:16.920
as it called cough and
cold and breathlessness,

00:41:16.920 --> 00:41:17.753
which would, of course,

00:41:17.753 --> 00:41:20.280
symptoms of this respiratory disorder

00:41:20.280 --> 00:41:22.110
that we understood COVID to be.

00:41:22.110 --> 00:41:23.610
But had they been tested, no.

00:41:23.610 --> 00:41:25.050
And because they hadn't been tested,

00:41:25.050 --> 00:41:27.390
they would never be
counted among the dead.

00:41:27.390 --> 00:41:30.750
And because rural India was
completely invisibilized,

00:41:30.750 --> 00:41:32.730
nobody was documenting these.

00:41:32.730 --> 00:41:35.910
So, you were not counted
if you died at home,

00:41:35.910 --> 00:41:39.360
you were not counted if you
did not get an RT-PCR Test.

00:41:39.360 --> 00:41:44.360
I have met people who spent
their days in an ambulance

00:41:45.120 --> 00:41:47.100
trying to get tested,
and couldn't get tested,

00:41:47.100 --> 00:41:48.570
and then died in the ambulance.

00:41:48.570 --> 00:41:50.640
That would not be
counted as a COVID death.

00:41:50.640 --> 00:41:51.930
And you were not counted as dead

00:41:51.930 --> 00:41:53.970
if you died from shortage of oxygen,

00:41:53.970 --> 00:41:56.640
which scores of people dead

00:41:56.640 --> 00:41:58.860
as oxygen just ran out in the ICUs.

00:41:59.893 --> 00:42:03.830
And if your death certificate
did not list COVID

00:42:03.830 --> 00:42:04.830
as the cause of death,

00:42:04.830 --> 00:42:07.380
and there were states where
the local administration

00:42:07.380 --> 00:42:09.750
was instructed that you don't
get into the cause of death,

00:42:09.750 --> 00:42:11.130
let the hospital decide.

00:42:11.130 --> 00:42:13.380
The hospital would say,
oh, oxygen ran short,

00:42:13.380 --> 00:42:14.940
but I'm not gonna give
that as the cause of death.

00:42:14.940 --> 00:42:18.180
Cause of death was
cardiac disorder, right?

00:42:18.180 --> 00:42:21.450
So through the cracks of officialdom,

00:42:21.450 --> 00:42:22.860
the way the system was set up,

00:42:22.860 --> 00:42:25.230
the fact that many people
couldn't get a death certificate,

00:42:25.230 --> 00:42:26.490
the fact that the cause of death

00:42:26.490 --> 00:42:27.990
became something that the administration,

00:42:27.990 --> 00:42:28.830
and private hospitals

00:42:28.830 --> 00:42:30.720
started bombing off to one another.

00:42:30.720 --> 00:42:32.850
And again, remember if
you think of Mukesh Mandal

00:42:32.850 --> 00:42:34.200
who had sold his phone,

00:42:34.200 --> 00:42:36.630
now you had objectively
poor people who sold

00:42:36.630 --> 00:42:38.910
again everything they own,
maybe a piece of jewelry,

00:42:38.910 --> 00:42:40.800
maybe a piece of land

00:42:40.800 --> 00:42:44.040
in the belief that a private
hospital would save lives.

00:42:44.040 --> 00:42:45.660
They thought, okay,

00:42:45.660 --> 00:42:48.270
public health system
is totally overridden,

00:42:48.270 --> 00:42:50.400
maybe the private hospitals will.

00:42:50.400 --> 00:42:52.740
And that in so many of
these private hospitals

00:42:52.740 --> 00:42:54.570
the oxygen ran shot.

00:42:54.570 --> 00:42:57.060
In the meantime, there was
some family that was so poor

00:42:57.060 --> 00:43:00.120
that they did not even
have wood to burn the dead.

00:43:00.120 --> 00:43:02.190
So for a combination of these reasons,

00:43:02.190 --> 00:43:05.490
we started seeing this very
tragic phenomena in North India,

00:43:05.490 --> 00:43:06.480
in parts of North India,

00:43:06.480 --> 00:43:09.240
where bodies were thrown into rivers

00:43:09.240 --> 00:43:11.850
or left in the river
banks in the dead of night

00:43:11.850 --> 00:43:14.100
with just what is called
the ramnami cloth,

00:43:14.100 --> 00:43:16.143
which is a sacred cloth for Hindus.

00:43:17.430 --> 00:43:20.350
And this is the kind of
scene that you would see

00:43:21.570 --> 00:43:25.140
as you travel down the banks of the river,

00:43:25.140 --> 00:43:29.220
but this became a really,
really politicized conversation.

00:43:29.220 --> 00:43:31.203
And by 2021,

00:43:32.575 --> 00:43:35.820
I started to feel a kind of
fury at everything I was seeing.

00:43:35.820 --> 00:43:39.090
And I'm a political junkie.
I live for elections.

00:43:39.090 --> 00:43:40.950
I live to cover elections.

00:43:40.950 --> 00:43:45.950
But at that time, it seemed
to me to be so, I don't know,

00:43:46.200 --> 00:43:49.320
immortal, and egregious that
elections were being held

00:43:49.320 --> 00:43:52.260
while this is what I was
finding on the banks,

00:43:52.260 --> 00:43:53.580
that elections were being held

00:43:53.580 --> 00:43:57.090
while schools were closed, right?

00:43:57.090 --> 00:44:00.810
That government employees
who were teachers

00:44:00.810 --> 00:44:03.390
in government schools were forcibly sent

00:44:03.390 --> 00:44:06.600
on election duty for local elections.

00:44:06.600 --> 00:44:09.270
And more than 1000 of
them died from COVID.

00:44:09.270 --> 00:44:12.540
These were all lower income
families who were employed.

00:44:12.540 --> 00:44:14.430
A pregnant woman was among them.

00:44:14.430 --> 00:44:18.630
So I was for the first
time in my democracy

00:44:18.630 --> 00:44:19.920
that I'm otherwise proud of,

00:44:19.920 --> 00:44:23.640
I was really offended by the
fact that these elections

00:44:23.640 --> 00:44:28.053
were being held, and I decided
to not cover the elections.

00:44:28.920 --> 00:44:30.300
Maybe it made a difference to nobody,

00:44:30.300 --> 00:44:32.790
but at my level boycotted,

00:44:32.790 --> 00:44:34.500
and I don't really believe
in boycott, but I was like,

00:44:34.500 --> 00:44:35.610
I'm not gonna cover these elections.

00:44:35.610 --> 00:44:37.923
These elections shouldn't be held.

00:44:37.923 --> 00:44:39.450
So there was again in
this media disconnect

00:44:39.450 --> 00:44:42.540
because by now the media
had just turned away

00:44:42.540 --> 00:44:45.150
from all of this, and was
back on the elections.

00:44:45.150 --> 00:44:47.100
And the elections were the headlines.

00:44:47.100 --> 00:44:49.140
These were not the headlines, right?

00:44:49.140 --> 00:44:52.590
And this is what was happening
on the banks of our river.

00:44:52.590 --> 00:44:55.980
Many of these places were
not reachable by road.

00:44:55.980 --> 00:44:59.640
You had to get onto a
motorcycle, you had to walk,

00:44:59.640 --> 00:45:04.380
you had to take a boat down the Ganga.

00:45:04.380 --> 00:45:09.380
And finally, we said in the beginning that

00:45:11.430 --> 00:45:15.390
every existing inequity
was exacerbated by COVID.

00:45:15.390 --> 00:45:16.710
Among the things I discovered

00:45:16.710 --> 00:45:19.170
was what happened to cast groups

00:45:19.170 --> 00:45:20.400
whose traditional occupation

00:45:20.400 --> 00:45:22.950
was to wash the bodies of the dead.

00:45:22.950 --> 00:45:26.130
And these cast groups
existed even within Muslims.

00:45:26.130 --> 00:45:29.317
So this was Hyderabad
where I met a man who said,

00:45:29.317 --> 00:45:30.150
"I'm a body washer."

00:45:30.150 --> 00:45:31.590
That was his professional occupation.

00:45:31.590 --> 00:45:33.930
He actually had an ID card that said this.

00:45:33.930 --> 00:45:36.210
And nobody was willing to
come near these families.

00:45:36.210 --> 00:45:39.660
And in Varanasi where Hindus

00:45:39.660 --> 00:45:43.230
among Hindus Dalits
traditionally washed the body,

00:45:43.230 --> 00:45:44.520
and were washing COVID bodies

00:45:44.520 --> 00:45:46.270
before they were taken to the pyre.

00:45:48.450 --> 00:45:49.800
I remember what one of them said,

00:45:49.800 --> 00:45:50.880
and this happened in front of me.

00:45:50.880 --> 00:45:52.677
I was walking with him in
front of a cremation ground,

00:45:52.677 --> 00:45:54.427
and there was a shopkeeper and he said,

00:45:54.427 --> 00:45:55.620
"Get away from here."

00:45:55.620 --> 00:45:57.210
He said to this young man

00:45:57.210 --> 00:45:59.370
who's washing everybody's bodies

00:45:59.370 --> 00:46:00.630
before they they were lit.

00:46:00.630 --> 00:46:01.800
And he said, "Look at this.

00:46:01.800 --> 00:46:05.370
We are doing what families want,

00:46:05.370 --> 00:46:06.930
but we are still treated like this."

00:46:06.930 --> 00:46:09.360
And now doubly so because we are washing

00:46:09.360 --> 00:46:11.460
the bodies of the COVID death.

00:46:11.460 --> 00:46:14.790
So I guess in effect,

00:46:14.790 --> 00:46:17.100
there were things of
course that were uplifting.

00:46:17.100 --> 00:46:21.390
They were for the poison that
we saw on television channels.

00:46:21.390 --> 00:46:24.690
I met community groups where
Hindus, and Muslims buried,

00:46:24.690 --> 00:46:29.670
and cremated each other's
sort of each other's dead.

00:46:29.670 --> 00:46:33.060
I met a man called Abdul
Malbar in Surat in Gujarat

00:46:33.060 --> 00:46:35.797
where he had one ambulance
and the ambulance said,

00:46:35.797 --> 00:46:37.620
"Funerals for lonely humans."

00:46:37.620 --> 00:46:40.650
And his one purpose in life was to go,

00:46:40.650 --> 00:46:44.490
and according to your faith
perform the last rites.

00:46:44.490 --> 00:46:49.490
So in his sort of closet sized office,

00:46:50.079 --> 00:46:51.990
he had Ganga jar,

00:46:51.990 --> 00:46:53.760
which is a sacred water
of the river Ganga,

00:46:53.760 --> 00:46:55.530
but he also had namaz mats.

00:46:55.530 --> 00:46:57.333
In the Housing Societies in Surat,

00:46:58.380 --> 00:47:01.087
I met women who were homemakers who said,

00:47:01.087 --> 00:47:02.370
"We don't know how to help,

00:47:02.370 --> 00:47:05.460
but maybe we can cook five
more rotis than we do,

00:47:05.460 --> 00:47:07.620
and every day we cook these rotis."

00:47:07.620 --> 00:47:10.980
We saw people rally together
and behind each other

00:47:10.980 --> 00:47:12.900
help perfect strangers.

00:47:12.900 --> 00:47:15.090
And when we finally got our act together

00:47:15.090 --> 00:47:16.680
in terms of getting the vaccines out,

00:47:16.680 --> 00:47:20.400
as I said in the beginning, we did well,

00:47:20.400 --> 00:47:22.800
we had very minimal vaccine hesitancy

00:47:22.800 --> 00:47:25.600
compared to the kind of
politics you see around it here.

00:47:27.136 --> 00:47:29.880
Once we learned from our
mistakes, we learned,

00:47:29.880 --> 00:47:31.980
but by then so many people

00:47:31.980 --> 00:47:33.900
had paid a very, very costly price.

00:47:33.900 --> 00:47:36.750
And I think, I'll just end by saying like,

00:47:36.750 --> 00:47:38.730
I know it's difficult to remember COVID.

00:47:38.730 --> 00:47:42.000
I know that people don't want
to remember it, but again,

00:47:42.000 --> 00:47:46.050
I'll drop part with
partition where Ashis Nandy,

00:47:46.050 --> 00:47:48.240
who's one of India's best sociologists,

00:47:48.240 --> 00:47:50.970
he did this project called
Memory and Partition.

00:47:50.970 --> 00:47:53.130
And one of the things he argued was that

00:47:53.130 --> 00:47:54.720
whenever something cataclysmic happens

00:47:54.720 --> 00:47:55.770
in the life of a people,

00:47:55.770 --> 00:47:59.010
or in the life of a nation
like the partition of India,

00:47:59.010 --> 00:48:02.970
the instinct is to put a lid
on it, and to bury the memory.

00:48:02.970 --> 00:48:04.770
And the moment you bury a memory,

00:48:04.770 --> 00:48:07.710
there is a kind of
schizophrenia that develops,

00:48:07.710 --> 00:48:10.860
and that has developed as we
see between India and Pakistan.

00:48:10.860 --> 00:48:12.270
And COVID is something similar.

00:48:12.270 --> 00:48:14.910
I think we are desperate to forget.

00:48:14.910 --> 00:48:16.080
I can understand that,

00:48:16.080 --> 00:48:18.903
I live nothing else for two years plus.

00:48:19.980 --> 00:48:22.050
I get the whole need to move on.

00:48:22.050 --> 00:48:24.240
But I think if we don't pause to think,

00:48:24.240 --> 00:48:26.820
okay, what did it teach
us about ourselves?

00:48:26.820 --> 00:48:28.410
What did it teach us
about our relationships?

00:48:28.410 --> 00:48:29.670
What did it teach us about society,

00:48:29.670 --> 00:48:32.700
about the organization of
power, about, I don't know,

00:48:32.700 --> 00:48:33.900
access to resources?

00:48:33.900 --> 00:48:36.033
What should policy be going forward?

00:48:37.756 --> 00:48:39.540
Why aren't these things
that people vote on?

00:48:39.540 --> 00:48:42.120
The quality of air, the
quality of healthcare.

00:48:42.120 --> 00:48:44.100
There's so much to think about here?

00:48:44.100 --> 00:48:46.740
And for me personally I call

00:48:46.740 --> 00:48:48.630
reporting the Kargil War in '99.

00:48:48.630 --> 00:48:52.260
My first masterclass in
life, I was 26 and a half,

00:48:52.260 --> 00:48:54.300
I covered COVID in my 40s.

00:48:54.300 --> 00:48:56.820
And it really literally was
a second masterclass in life.

00:48:56.820 --> 00:48:59.520
It made me reexamine every assumption

00:48:59.520 --> 00:49:02.550
of what I understood to be
the role of a journalist,

00:49:02.550 --> 00:49:04.320
what I understood to be my role,

00:49:04.320 --> 00:49:06.780
what I understood to be
the hierarchy of news,

00:49:06.780 --> 00:49:08.640
what I understood about TV channels

00:49:08.640 --> 00:49:10.636
about the people of India.

00:49:10.636 --> 00:49:12.603
And I think those discussions continue.

00:49:14.130 --> 00:49:16.080
Many things don't have certitude,

00:49:16.080 --> 00:49:18.720
but as long as one can at least
begin asking the questions

00:49:18.720 --> 00:49:20.760
now with the benefit of some distance,

00:49:20.760 --> 00:49:23.730
I think that will be the
lesson we need to take away,

00:49:23.730 --> 00:49:25.890
otherwise it may not be COVID,

00:49:25.890 --> 00:49:27.570
it'll be something else tomorrow,

00:49:27.570 --> 00:49:30.810
and our fault clients will
be wrenched open once again.

00:49:30.810 --> 00:49:32.989
So I'll just stop there.
Thank you very much.

00:49:32.989 --> 00:49:35.989
(audience applauds)

00:49:40.485 --> 00:49:41.670
- Thank you, Barkha.

00:49:41.670 --> 00:49:44.850
That was all absorbing
and also heart wrenching,

00:49:44.850 --> 00:49:46.410
and I think I speak for everyone,

00:49:46.410 --> 00:49:48.060
we're sorry for your loss
as well of your father

00:49:48.060 --> 00:49:49.260
through all this,

00:49:49.260 --> 00:49:50.340
but thank you for sharing that,

00:49:50.340 --> 00:49:52.680
and for taking that journey
over those two years as well.

00:49:52.680 --> 00:49:55.140
I think you did a huge
service for all of us

00:49:55.140 --> 00:49:57.393
to really understand what has happened.

00:49:58.230 --> 00:50:00.660
I'm going to turn right now,
and I hope that this works

00:50:00.660 --> 00:50:04.410
if I just move ahead with the slides, yes.

00:50:04.410 --> 00:50:06.900
To some research insights.

00:50:06.900 --> 00:50:08.580
So I'm going to try to put some data

00:50:08.580 --> 00:50:11.040
to some of the faces that you showed us.

00:50:11.040 --> 00:50:13.080
And I want to turn to the issue of gender.

00:50:13.080 --> 00:50:14.520
And I know we don't have a lot of time,

00:50:14.520 --> 00:50:16.260
so I'm going to ask two questions,

00:50:16.260 --> 00:50:18.150
and then I think we can
open it up for the audience,

00:50:18.150 --> 00:50:19.530
and the people online as well,

00:50:19.530 --> 00:50:21.630
so we make sure we can
have time with them.

00:50:21.630 --> 00:50:23.580
So in July when we were together,

00:50:23.580 --> 00:50:25.380
we talked a little bit
about some of the surveys

00:50:25.380 --> 00:50:27.660
that we've conducted
through inclusion economics.

00:50:27.660 --> 00:50:30.390
So that's, lemme come a
little closer, thanks,

00:50:30.390 --> 00:50:33.960
that's with Rohini here, Jenna Allard,

00:50:33.960 --> 00:50:35.520
who's in the audience, myself,

00:50:35.520 --> 00:50:36.997
Yusuf Neggers, Simone Schaner,

00:50:36.997 --> 00:50:39.960
and then Maulik Jagnani here
with other universities.

00:50:39.960 --> 00:50:43.380
So when COVID hit, we
decided what can we do?

00:50:43.380 --> 00:50:44.940
I think like many
people, we were thinking,

00:50:44.940 --> 00:50:46.470
what can we do to help, right?

00:50:46.470 --> 00:50:48.780
And what we knew we could
do is we can collect data,

00:50:48.780 --> 00:50:50.220
and at least try to document,

00:50:50.220 --> 00:50:53.610
and then share information about
the migrants in particular,

00:50:53.610 --> 00:50:55.650
but just anyone that we
could collect data on

00:50:55.650 --> 00:50:58.020
to understand what was
happening through this crisis.

00:50:58.020 --> 00:51:00.067
And we worked with government
partners and they said,

00:51:00.067 --> 00:51:02.760
"We don't really understand
what's happening,

00:51:02.760 --> 00:51:03.840
so please do help us,

00:51:03.840 --> 00:51:05.760
help us understand in
particular these migrants

00:51:05.760 --> 00:51:07.020
that are leaving cities."

00:51:07.020 --> 00:51:10.380
And so the migrants as
they were returning back

00:51:10.380 --> 00:51:12.090
to their home states
they were intercepted,

00:51:12.090 --> 00:51:13.680
and people would collect their details,

00:51:13.680 --> 00:51:16.590
and then we use that information
to call these individuals.

00:51:16.590 --> 00:51:17.640
And ultimately what we did

00:51:17.640 --> 00:51:20.520
is we went back to a
core group of migrants

00:51:20.520 --> 00:51:23.070
about four different times to understand

00:51:23.070 --> 00:51:25.410
how well they were doing
through the pandemic.

00:51:25.410 --> 00:51:27.090
So just to kind of show you,

00:51:27.090 --> 00:51:29.520
we surveyed them over a 15 month period.

00:51:29.520 --> 00:51:31.680
We reached out to them
four different times,

00:51:31.680 --> 00:51:34.980
and had 1,780 individuals
that returned to Bihar,

00:51:34.980 --> 00:51:37.140
and Chhattisgarh to just understand

00:51:37.140 --> 00:51:38.370
what had happened to them.

00:51:38.370 --> 00:51:40.980
And what came out from this in particular

00:51:40.980 --> 00:51:43.980
was a real gender gap,
and gender disparities

00:51:43.980 --> 00:51:47.880
in terms of being able to
return to life as usual,

00:51:47.880 --> 00:51:50.940
particularly for those who had
worked prior to the pandemic.

00:51:50.940 --> 00:51:53.190
So I'm gonna focus in
on the group of migrants

00:51:53.190 --> 00:51:54.120
who are already working.

00:51:54.120 --> 00:51:56.010
So these were, many
women in India don't work

00:51:56.010 --> 00:51:57.810
of course outside the home,

00:51:57.810 --> 00:51:59.700
but we're only focusing on men,

00:51:59.700 --> 00:52:01.740
and women who worked
prior to the pandemic.

00:52:01.740 --> 00:52:04.320
And what I'll show you
is we have information,

00:52:04.320 --> 00:52:06.270
sorry, it's gonna be
hard for you to see here,

00:52:06.270 --> 00:52:09.360
but at the beginning of the pandemic

00:52:09.360 --> 00:52:12.000
we're looking at all women
who were working about four,

00:52:12.000 --> 00:52:14.700
and five were working in
some sort of unskilled labor,

00:52:14.700 --> 00:52:15.600
and we surveyed them

00:52:15.600 --> 00:52:17.970
through what we called recovery
phase one, and phase two,

00:52:17.970 --> 00:52:19.470
and then in COVID phase two.

00:52:19.470 --> 00:52:21.150
And what we saw is that these women

00:52:21.150 --> 00:52:23.310
ended up going from skilled labor

00:52:23.310 --> 00:52:25.140
often into unskilled labor

00:52:25.140 --> 00:52:26.940
or more commonly agricultural jobs

00:52:26.940 --> 00:52:29.640
or just dropping outta
the labor force entirely.

00:52:29.640 --> 00:52:31.800
And that persisted all
the way through the end

00:52:31.800 --> 00:52:33.150
of the second phase of COVID

00:52:33.150 --> 00:52:35.070
where half of the women that we spoke with

00:52:35.070 --> 00:52:37.650
who had previously been
employed in urban areas,

00:52:37.650 --> 00:52:39.210
they were no longer employed.

00:52:39.210 --> 00:52:42.630
Many of these women were stuck
back at home in the villages,

00:52:42.630 --> 00:52:44.310
and could not actually return.

00:52:44.310 --> 00:52:47.040
If we compare it to the figure for men

00:52:47.040 --> 00:52:49.080
what we see here is about half of the men

00:52:49.080 --> 00:52:52.260
were in skilled labor, and then
as the pandemic hit, again,

00:52:52.260 --> 00:52:54.240
agriculture absorbed lots of migrants,

00:52:54.240 --> 00:52:55.320
and was able to help them

00:52:55.320 --> 00:52:57.150
especially through that first wave,

00:52:57.150 --> 00:53:00.240
and by the end, and by the second wave

00:53:00.240 --> 00:53:02.130
about a third of the men were unemployed

00:53:02.130 --> 00:53:03.840
even during this downturn,

00:53:03.840 --> 00:53:05.220
but it didn't really compare well

00:53:05.220 --> 00:53:07.470
to what was going on for women.

00:53:07.470 --> 00:53:08.400
And as I mentioned, sorry,

00:53:08.400 --> 00:53:10.560
this is probably too much
information on one side,

00:53:10.560 --> 00:53:13.380
but essentially women
were stuck in rural areas.

00:53:13.380 --> 00:53:15.570
The ones that were
remaining in rural areas

00:53:15.570 --> 00:53:17.340
had a much more difficult time

00:53:17.340 --> 00:53:19.530
actually finding work in particular.

00:53:19.530 --> 00:53:23.130
And this was 15 months
after the pandemic began,

00:53:23.130 --> 00:53:24.180
and that's what we see.

00:53:24.180 --> 00:53:27.960
We also see that women failed
to recover their earnings

00:53:27.960 --> 00:53:29.280
even prior to the pandemic,

00:53:29.280 --> 00:53:31.860
of course, they were earning
less money than men on average,

00:53:31.860 --> 00:53:33.270
but if we look at what happened to them,

00:53:33.270 --> 00:53:34.710
sorry for the blurriness at the bottom,

00:53:34.710 --> 00:53:36.540
I'm not sure why that is.

00:53:36.540 --> 00:53:39.210
If we look at their recovery,

00:53:39.210 --> 00:53:42.060
essentially men were able to recover

00:53:42.060 --> 00:53:43.740
very close to the level that they earned

00:53:43.740 --> 00:53:44.760
prior to the pandemic,

00:53:44.760 --> 00:53:47.730
but these women, again,
were not able to do that.

00:53:47.730 --> 00:53:49.770
And then finally, these women too,

00:53:49.770 --> 00:53:52.530
particularly the ones that were
kind of stuck in rural areas

00:53:52.530 --> 00:53:54.360
15 months after the pandemic began

00:53:54.360 --> 00:53:57.240
were reporting that they were
likely to reduce consumption,

00:53:57.240 --> 00:53:59.370
and more so than kind of other men,

00:53:59.370 --> 00:54:03.090
and or men, and those who had
gone into urban areas as well.

00:54:03.090 --> 00:54:04.050
So, all that to say,

00:54:04.050 --> 00:54:06.690
just wanted to show you some
of what we had gathered,

00:54:06.690 --> 00:54:08.790
and I'd love to hear your
perspective in particular

00:54:08.790 --> 00:54:10.917
on what happened to women
through the pandemic,

00:54:10.917 --> 00:54:13.380
and what you saw through that gender lens.

00:54:13.380 --> 00:54:17.785
- Yeah, I mean, I think that as you said

00:54:17.785 --> 00:54:20.070
(indistinct) inequity amplified,

00:54:20.070 --> 00:54:23.550
and that is what happened
to women as well.

00:54:23.550 --> 00:54:28.550
And what I remember is during
the pandemic pregnant women

00:54:29.490 --> 00:54:32.370
really struggling to get
access to any healthcare,

00:54:32.370 --> 00:54:34.320
that is my first memory of COVID.

00:54:34.320 --> 00:54:37.410
Like you actually had to
have doctors going to slums

00:54:37.410 --> 00:54:39.210
or do these virtual calls,

00:54:39.210 --> 00:54:41.400
and you would meet these
pregnant women on the streets.

00:54:41.400 --> 00:54:44.130
When we spoke about the other
people who needed healthcare.

00:54:44.130 --> 00:54:45.810
So that is my first memory,

00:54:45.810 --> 00:54:46.830
think of all these pregnant women.

00:54:46.830 --> 00:54:50.490
Secondly, in India at least,
I don't know how it was here,

00:54:50.490 --> 00:54:53.820
pregnant women got
access to vaccines last.

00:54:53.820 --> 00:54:55.380
The debate just carried on over

00:54:55.380 --> 00:54:57.120
whether it was safe, not safe.

00:54:57.120 --> 00:55:00.540
And finally it was like a
group of sort of doctors

00:55:00.540 --> 00:55:04.170
that led the demand, and said
that this must must happen.

00:55:04.170 --> 00:55:07.050
But what happened inside homes

00:55:07.050 --> 00:55:10.140
is in fact very, very distressing.

00:55:10.140 --> 00:55:12.660
We had a distinct spike

00:55:12.660 --> 00:55:15.840
in the reported cases of domestic abuse.

00:55:15.840 --> 00:55:17.670
We don't even know right now

00:55:17.670 --> 00:55:20.310
what the unreported
cases may have been like.

00:55:20.310 --> 00:55:22.200
The government run child helpline

00:55:22.200 --> 00:55:26.010
was deluged with calls from children,

00:55:26.010 --> 00:55:28.830
and girls mostly saying that we do not,

00:55:28.830 --> 00:55:30.993
we are being forced
into underage marriage.

00:55:32.070 --> 00:55:35.280
I went to investigate one of these trends,

00:55:35.280 --> 00:55:37.350
and I ended up in a village in Rajasthan

00:55:37.350 --> 00:55:40.410
where I basically found that
what was happening was that

00:55:40.410 --> 00:55:43.200
because we had among the longest

00:55:43.200 --> 00:55:45.220
physical school lockdowns in the world

00:55:46.830 --> 00:55:49.800
vast number of girls were
just out of the classroom.

00:55:49.800 --> 00:55:52.950
If a family had one internet device

00:55:52.950 --> 00:55:55.650
then if there was a boy
it was given to the boy.

00:55:55.650 --> 00:55:59.640
And so you had all of these
girls who had nothing to do,

00:55:59.640 --> 00:56:01.110
and then their families would tell them

00:56:01.110 --> 00:56:02.850
that since you're doing nothing,

00:56:02.850 --> 00:56:04.620
you should go and get married.

00:56:04.620 --> 00:56:07.740
And one of the saddest things I ever heard

00:56:07.740 --> 00:56:08.970
was one of the activists

00:56:08.970 --> 00:56:10.350
who was working with these girls told me

00:56:10.350 --> 00:56:11.940
that in some cases it's the mothers

00:56:11.940 --> 00:56:13.860
who are pushing for these girls

00:56:13.860 --> 00:56:18.420
to go to the so-called
infant husband's village

00:56:18.420 --> 00:56:20.880
because with the man at home

00:56:20.880 --> 00:56:22.900
if he turned abusive or violent

00:56:24.309 --> 00:56:29.309
or in some cases forced
sex on his partner,

00:56:29.460 --> 00:56:31.680
and remember these are
homes we're talking about

00:56:31.680 --> 00:56:34.440
in very enclosed tiny spaces,

00:56:34.440 --> 00:56:37.290
the mothers didn't think their
daughters were safe at all.

00:56:37.290 --> 00:56:39.000
And they actually thought
that the daughters

00:56:39.000 --> 00:56:40.050
may be safer if...

00:56:40.050 --> 00:56:41.790
So, some did it because what you're doing?

00:56:41.790 --> 00:56:43.140
Go, get married.

00:56:43.140 --> 00:56:44.193
Some did it because they thought

00:56:44.193 --> 00:56:46.230
that they were actually creating distance

00:56:46.230 --> 00:56:49.230
between their girls in an abusive house.

00:56:49.230 --> 00:56:51.540
Of course, like with
everything else in India,

00:56:51.540 --> 00:56:53.930
these girls answered it by
forming a football team.

00:56:53.930 --> 00:56:55.530
It was just an incredible story.

00:56:55.530 --> 00:56:56.550
They formed a football team,

00:56:56.550 --> 00:56:58.417
and every time the
village elders would say,

00:56:58.417 --> 00:56:59.250
"What are you doing?"

00:56:59.250 --> 00:57:00.330
They say we play football.

00:57:00.330 --> 00:57:02.490
And then they actually became
state level football players,

00:57:02.490 --> 00:57:06.960
but not every village had that
kind of sort of happy ending.

00:57:06.960 --> 00:57:09.810
And let's not pretend that
this happened only to the poor.

00:57:09.810 --> 00:57:11.820
We had, I think, if I'm not wrong,

00:57:11.820 --> 00:57:14.580
please you guys are the
experts, at some point

00:57:14.580 --> 00:57:17.862
I think the female labor force
participation number hit 9%

00:57:17.862 --> 00:57:19.312
at one point in the pandemic.

00:57:20.190 --> 00:57:21.900
And basically what was happening

00:57:21.900 --> 00:57:23.820
was that even in the paleo class,

00:57:23.820 --> 00:57:27.300
people like us with partners at home,

00:57:27.300 --> 00:57:31.290
with children at home this work from home

00:57:31.290 --> 00:57:32.670
today sounds like a concept

00:57:32.670 --> 00:57:34.110
which is meant to make your life easier.

00:57:34.110 --> 00:57:36.060
But if you're a woman in India,

00:57:36.060 --> 00:57:39.900
and there is no equality at home,

00:57:39.900 --> 00:57:42.150
there's no degenerating of the workspace,

00:57:42.150 --> 00:57:45.540
of the home space, I beg your
pardon, of the roles at home,

00:57:45.540 --> 00:57:47.580
of whose job it is to put
the food on the table,

00:57:47.580 --> 00:57:49.620
and whose job it is to
get the children dressed,

00:57:49.620 --> 00:57:52.980
and whose job it is to
also do your job, right?

00:57:52.980 --> 00:57:56.130
That burden fell (indistinct) on women,

00:57:56.130 --> 00:57:57.570
and women just stopped walking.

00:57:57.570 --> 00:57:59.520
Women just said, "We can't do this."

00:57:59.520 --> 00:58:02.850
So, everything from vaccine access

00:58:02.850 --> 00:58:04.800
to access to healthcare for pregnant women

00:58:04.800 --> 00:58:07.800
to domestic violence to underage marriages

00:58:07.800 --> 00:58:09.333
to having to leave your work.

00:58:10.440 --> 00:58:14.460
This was a very, very,
very gendered experience.

00:58:14.460 --> 00:58:17.223
And the other thing is that women,

00:58:18.600 --> 00:58:21.000
at least in our cultural context,

00:58:21.000 --> 00:58:23.970
are so conditioned to be all sacrificing.

00:58:23.970 --> 00:58:27.210
There is so much romance
around this sacrificing woman

00:58:27.210 --> 00:58:29.460
that you don't hear a lot of these stories

00:58:29.460 --> 00:58:30.690
till you go out to seek them.

00:58:30.690 --> 00:58:32.460
The women don't come forward to complain

00:58:32.460 --> 00:58:35.550
because they have over decades
of cultural conditioning

00:58:35.550 --> 00:58:38.400
internalized that if there's
one roti in the house,

00:58:38.400 --> 00:58:40.680
my child and my husband will eat it first.

00:58:40.680 --> 00:58:42.120
So I haven't even begun

00:58:42.120 --> 00:58:44.480
to get into sort of the
malnourishment data,

00:58:44.480 --> 00:58:47.070
or the maternal mortality
data that would've emerged

00:58:47.070 --> 00:58:48.180
in these years,

00:58:48.180 --> 00:58:51.870
but anecdotally this is
what I saw happening.

00:58:51.870 --> 00:58:52.980
I saw women leaving work

00:58:52.980 --> 00:58:56.970
in educated upper middle
classrooms and literally,

00:58:56.970 --> 00:59:00.330
I mean I know women when I
literally was like, don't do it.

00:59:00.330 --> 00:59:01.163
You'll regret it.

00:59:01.163 --> 00:59:02.730
It'll be tough to get back.

00:59:02.730 --> 00:59:04.890
It'll be just kind of
take the anxiety of it.

00:59:04.890 --> 00:59:08.940
So at every layer you saw
women experiencing the pandemic

00:59:08.940 --> 00:59:10.593
in a very, very different way.

00:59:12.480 --> 00:59:13.630
- Definitely resonates.

00:59:15.000 --> 00:59:17.010
Okay, so I have one last question for you.

00:59:17.010 --> 00:59:18.390
Thank you for that.

00:59:18.390 --> 00:59:19.980
And then we can open
it up to the audience,

00:59:19.980 --> 00:59:21.450
and people online too.

00:59:21.450 --> 00:59:23.550
And that is you referenced,

00:59:23.550 --> 00:59:25.470
I think we're probably the group of people

00:59:25.470 --> 00:59:26.430
that if we were in India,

00:59:26.430 --> 00:59:28.170
we were baking banana bread,

00:59:28.170 --> 00:59:30.240
and maybe sending some money to someone

00:59:30.240 --> 00:59:32.490
or in the U.S. making sourdough bread

00:59:32.490 --> 00:59:35.910
or whatever it was,
and you told this story

00:59:35.910 --> 00:59:38.130
about this young boy,
what does corona mean?

00:59:38.130 --> 00:59:40.440
What is corona? It was,
it means that I'm hungry.

00:59:40.440 --> 00:59:44.520
So, COVID's not the last
shock we're all going to face.

00:59:44.520 --> 00:59:46.860
And I think, you said you
want us to learn from this,

00:59:46.860 --> 00:59:50.010
so, just practically
speaking, what can we do?

00:59:50.010 --> 00:59:52.440
How can we help consider

00:59:52.440 --> 00:59:55.290
those that are completely invisibilized,

00:59:55.290 --> 00:59:57.300
and marginalized in the day to day?

00:59:57.300 --> 00:59:58.440
Yeah.

00:59:58.440 --> 01:00:00.300
- I mean, to be completely honest

01:00:00.300 --> 01:00:01.410
if I weren't a journalist,

01:00:01.410 --> 01:00:02.760
I would've also been
at home with the same,

01:00:02.760 --> 01:00:04.650
I come from the same class,
I come from privilege.

01:00:04.650 --> 01:00:08.040
So I may self-identify as middle class,

01:00:08.040 --> 01:00:12.454
but that is more cultural value
than an economic condition.

01:00:12.454 --> 01:00:13.980
And so I don't want to be,

01:00:13.980 --> 01:00:17.460
I wasn't meaning to be holier
than thou or being judgy.

01:00:17.460 --> 01:00:19.230
I was just saying that
was the fact of life

01:00:19.230 --> 01:00:20.400
that upper middle class Indians

01:00:20.400 --> 01:00:21.630
were distraught with anxiety,

01:00:21.630 --> 01:00:22.500
and they were dealing with it

01:00:22.500 --> 01:00:24.600
by extending themselves
with all kinds of new

01:00:24.600 --> 01:00:25.830
sort of cooking skills or whatever.

01:00:25.830 --> 01:00:26.663
And I would've done the same

01:00:26.663 --> 01:00:29.910
had I've been sitting at home
and not been among the people.

01:00:29.910 --> 01:00:32.490
I basically think we have to ask more

01:00:32.490 --> 01:00:34.740
of our political class and our media.

01:00:34.740 --> 01:00:37.860
As people on this side,
you have to ask more of it.

01:00:37.860 --> 01:00:40.740
You see right now what really scares me

01:00:40.740 --> 01:00:43.677
is that there is a growing
disconnect between people

01:00:43.677 --> 01:00:45.510
and politics and people and media.

01:00:45.510 --> 01:00:47.070
And I think a lot of people,

01:00:47.070 --> 01:00:49.260
like I think (indistinct) had this study

01:00:49.260 --> 01:00:51.327
where they spoke about
this new genre of people

01:00:51.327 --> 01:00:52.680
who are news avoiders,

01:00:52.680 --> 01:00:54.870
basically like growing number of people

01:00:54.870 --> 01:00:56.040
now feel there's no point

01:00:56.040 --> 01:00:58.440
by engaging with journalism because my,

01:00:58.440 --> 01:00:59.760
firstly, it's all bad news.

01:00:59.760 --> 01:01:02.850
I don't wanna feel bad or
I don't wanna be triggered

01:01:02.850 --> 01:01:05.520
or what can I do about it
anyway, so you know what?

01:01:05.520 --> 01:01:07.530
It's just better that I don't know it.

01:01:07.530 --> 01:01:08.700
And I have friends like this,

01:01:08.700 --> 01:01:12.360
we are friends who at the
end of like a day from hell,

01:01:12.360 --> 01:01:13.980
I'll go back and say,
don't you do this happened?

01:01:13.980 --> 01:01:14.970
And they'll be like, what?

01:01:14.970 --> 01:01:16.410
And in some ways I'm grateful for them

01:01:16.410 --> 01:01:17.430
because they keep me real,

01:01:17.430 --> 01:01:18.513
and there's another dimension to my life.

01:01:18.513 --> 01:01:20.220
It's the other level.

01:01:20.220 --> 01:01:24.240
It's instructive to me that
there is just such a gap

01:01:24.240 --> 01:01:27.750
that nobody today actually feels that

01:01:27.750 --> 01:01:31.230
what the media shows you is
something that impacts you.

01:01:31.230 --> 01:01:32.673
The media is just
something other people do.

01:01:32.673 --> 01:01:35.490
It's just some something
that journalists do.

01:01:35.490 --> 01:01:37.590
How does it impact your life, right?

01:01:37.590 --> 01:01:40.080
So I think till we start
asking more of our journalists,

01:01:40.080 --> 01:01:42.870
and more of our governments
everywhere in the world,

01:01:42.870 --> 01:01:45.393
I mean it really bothers me.

01:01:47.010 --> 01:01:49.170
As a person who lives in Delhi

01:01:49.170 --> 01:01:52.620
who has become asthmatic
in her 40s, that I am not,

01:01:52.620 --> 01:01:54.720
I live in a city where people don't vote

01:01:54.720 --> 01:01:59.340
on the basis of what
the quality of air is.

01:01:59.340 --> 01:02:01.770
That we as women don't vote on the basis

01:02:01.770 --> 01:02:04.410
of whom might have a policy

01:02:04.410 --> 01:02:07.500
that is more sort of forward thinking

01:02:07.500 --> 01:02:09.390
when it comes to women, right?

01:02:09.390 --> 01:02:11.040
And of course people are many things.

01:02:11.040 --> 01:02:14.098
We're not just women or
citizens of Delhi or just in,

01:02:14.098 --> 01:02:16.530
we are many different things and politics,

01:02:16.530 --> 01:02:20.790
elections are won, and lost
on a multiple set of issues.

01:02:20.790 --> 01:02:23.040
But what can we do is to not,

01:02:23.040 --> 01:02:25.840
I think the biggest thing we
can do is to not disengage.

01:02:26.778 --> 01:02:30.780
I just feel that there
is such a disengagement.

01:02:30.780 --> 01:02:33.270
It is so deep between
people, and politics,

01:02:33.270 --> 01:02:36.420
and people in media that I really believe

01:02:36.420 --> 01:02:39.420
that the next calamity, and
there will be a next calamity.

01:02:39.420 --> 01:02:42.513
I mean, would we have globally
learned anything from it?

01:02:43.710 --> 01:02:48.710
I remember I was looking
at the U.S. from Delhi,

01:02:49.380 --> 01:02:53.370
and I was like, how can you not have any,

01:02:53.370 --> 01:02:57.720
like how can there be such
tolerance for anti-vaccers?

01:02:57.720 --> 01:03:01.890
How self-indulgent is it that
people are dying in my country

01:03:01.890 --> 01:03:04.980
because they can't get access
to vaccines in the first stage

01:03:04.980 --> 01:03:07.230
when the government
hadn't purchased enough,

01:03:07.230 --> 01:03:09.420
and there are vaccines just going waste

01:03:09.420 --> 01:03:11.190
in other parts of the world, right?

01:03:11.190 --> 01:03:14.613
But who's engaged? Who's making a noise?

01:03:15.570 --> 01:03:16.737
And the second thing is,

01:03:16.737 --> 01:03:18.900
and I really believe
this is a storyteller,

01:03:18.900 --> 01:03:23.460
that you have to have a way to communicate

01:03:23.460 --> 01:03:24.900
outside of your echo chamber.

01:03:24.900 --> 01:03:27.780
There is no purpose in
my saying this to people

01:03:27.780 --> 01:03:31.410
who already think like me,
so then what is we achieved?

01:03:31.410 --> 01:03:34.380
And if it takes some
academic to get onto TikTok,

01:03:34.380 --> 01:03:36.960
if it takes me to learn
how to do a 30 second video

01:03:36.960 --> 01:03:40.853
instead of 40 minute talk, I
have to learn how to do it.

01:03:41.940 --> 01:03:45.180
We just have to get off our
preachy little high horses,

01:03:45.180 --> 01:03:48.240
and figure out a language
that actually speaks

01:03:48.240 --> 01:03:51.730
to people who are outside of how we think.

01:03:51.730 --> 01:03:54.240
And I think the collapse of conversation

01:03:54.240 --> 01:03:57.180
across differences of opinion in America,

01:03:57.180 --> 01:04:00.690
in India this is costly to framing policy

01:04:00.690 --> 01:04:02.250
because policy is for all,

01:04:02.250 --> 01:04:06.060
policy exists irrespective
of which party you vote,

01:04:06.060 --> 01:04:08.430
and therefore there has to be some way

01:04:08.430 --> 01:04:10.083
of finding consensus on policy.

01:04:10.980 --> 01:04:14.400
And with the polarization
in media, in politics,

01:04:14.400 --> 01:04:17.760
I really worry about finding
that consensus for policy.

01:04:17.760 --> 01:04:19.680
- Yeah, likewise.

01:04:19.680 --> 01:04:20.910
I definitely think that's an issue here.

01:04:20.910 --> 01:04:22.380
I know people have,

01:04:22.380 --> 01:04:23.730
I don't know if there's researchers

01:04:23.730 --> 01:04:25.530
that some story was even just hearing

01:04:25.530 --> 01:04:27.093
about family conversations,

01:04:28.080 --> 01:04:30.893
we even cartel those, and be
self censor I think a lot,

01:04:32.370 --> 01:04:33.573
and also disengage.

01:04:34.470 --> 01:04:37.140
Shall we go ahead with some
questions from the audience

01:04:37.140 --> 01:04:39.450
or Zara you kind of lead where we start.

01:04:39.450 --> 01:04:40.363
So does anyone here have questions?

01:04:40.363 --> 01:04:42.540
I think if you do go
ahead and raise your hand,

01:04:42.540 --> 01:04:46.290
and we've got a few from
the audience online as well.

01:04:48.420 --> 01:04:51.810
- Thank you first of all for
your time for coming here.

01:04:51.810 --> 01:04:56.810
I follow you, your channel on
YouTube and also on Facebook.

01:04:56.880 --> 01:04:59.670
So talking about holding
politicians accountable.

01:04:59.670 --> 01:05:02.520
I wholeheartedly agree with you on this.

01:05:02.520 --> 01:05:04.140
So I grew up in Kolkata,

01:05:04.140 --> 01:05:09.140
and I am very much in touch with

01:05:09.240 --> 01:05:10.380
everything that's going on in India,

01:05:10.380 --> 01:05:12.210
and especially in Bengal.

01:05:12.210 --> 01:05:16.847
So I begin my day with Andy TV and CNN,

01:05:16.847 --> 01:05:21.540
and I can confirm that
it's not the ideal way

01:05:21.540 --> 01:05:23.640
to start my day because it makes me angry.

01:05:25.080 --> 01:05:29.910
So last week I called while I
was watching the Indian news,

01:05:29.910 --> 01:05:32.670
I called the ABP Network.

01:05:32.670 --> 01:05:36.580
Kolkata based, because
a politician who I know

01:05:38.760 --> 01:05:40.170
had very humble beginnings,

01:05:40.170 --> 01:05:45.170
and really became Uber wealthy
in a few years as it happens.

01:05:45.990 --> 01:05:50.610
And he had an eye issue
that was bothering him,

01:05:50.610 --> 01:05:53.430
and like all politicians,
India wasn't good enough.

01:05:53.430 --> 01:05:57.990
So he flew to Johns Hopkins
to get his eye tested,

01:05:57.990 --> 01:05:59.820
and apparently he's also going to be back

01:05:59.820 --> 01:06:02.700
for a follow up appointment,

01:06:02.700 --> 01:06:04.350
and I can barely make it to New York City

01:06:04.350 --> 01:06:05.850
for my follow up appointments.

01:06:06.900 --> 01:06:11.317
I asked the person who
took the call, I said,

01:06:11.317 --> 01:06:14.700
"My complaint is not to
you not directed at you,

01:06:14.700 --> 01:06:17.400
but can you please ask your journalists

01:06:17.400 --> 01:06:19.380
to raise this question

01:06:19.380 --> 01:06:21.270
that when the public health infrastructure

01:06:21.270 --> 01:06:26.130
is barely existent, how on earth
do these people shamelessly

01:06:26.130 --> 01:06:29.340
fly all the way to the
U.S. for a eye exam?

01:06:29.340 --> 01:06:32.370
And why on the journalists
asking these questions?"

01:06:32.370 --> 01:06:37.370
And his answer was, "Ma'am you
know their hands are tied."

01:06:38.340 --> 01:06:41.040
And that is my request
to you first of all,

01:06:41.040 --> 01:06:43.410
thank you for choosing
independent journalism.

01:06:43.410 --> 01:06:47.250
I think it is very, very
important to keep democracy alive.

01:06:47.250 --> 01:06:52.250
And if you can address this
issue, and when you go back,

01:06:52.590 --> 01:06:57.590
and just bring some
awareness around this that...

01:06:58.380 --> 01:07:01.770
- Yeah, no thank you,
and I'll follow this up,

01:07:01.770 --> 01:07:04.140
but it's kind of similar in India,

01:07:04.140 --> 01:07:06.240
we're having this whole
debate around language,

01:07:06.240 --> 01:07:07.950
I don't know how many of
you have been following it,

01:07:07.950 --> 01:07:12.360
and a recent sort of parliamentary
panel recommended that,

01:07:12.360 --> 01:07:13.680
and parts of it has support

01:07:13.680 --> 01:07:16.350
an emphasis on regional languages

01:07:16.350 --> 01:07:19.380
on the fact that people should know Hindi,

01:07:19.380 --> 01:07:21.270
and also if you're in Tamil Nadu, Tamil,

01:07:21.270 --> 01:07:23.130
and just whatever your own language is,

01:07:23.130 --> 01:07:26.010
but the operative part
of it is this attempt

01:07:26.010 --> 01:07:27.840
to remove the privacy of English

01:07:27.840 --> 01:07:29.430
because it's a colonial language.

01:07:29.430 --> 01:07:33.780
And I just think that if
we were to just pull out

01:07:33.780 --> 01:07:34.650
how many people,

01:07:34.650 --> 01:07:36.000
how many of our political class

01:07:36.000 --> 01:07:38.910
send their children to
English medium schools,

01:07:38.910 --> 01:07:42.330
I think that the hypocrisy of
that would be evident to see.

01:07:42.330 --> 01:07:43.410
So I think it's similar.

01:07:43.410 --> 01:07:47.580
I think those who can get
treatment outside of India,

01:07:47.580 --> 01:07:51.240
in politics, in society,
even in media board,

01:07:51.240 --> 01:07:53.640
and I think, I mean
there's definitely a story

01:07:53.640 --> 01:07:55.440
if it's being done on taxpayers money,

01:07:55.440 --> 01:07:59.190
but otherwise it's a story
of double speak, right?

01:07:59.190 --> 01:08:00.420
Where you encourage the poor

01:08:00.420 --> 01:08:03.480
to say you must get treated at home,

01:08:03.480 --> 01:08:05.340
but you fly abroad yourself.

01:08:05.340 --> 01:08:08.040
But again you follow the news,

01:08:08.040 --> 01:08:10.620
but I'm telling you the real crisis is,

01:08:10.620 --> 01:08:12.720
one, people don't follow the news,

01:08:12.720 --> 01:08:14.220
two, when they follow the news,

01:08:14.220 --> 01:08:16.290
they only follow the news
that they already agree with.

01:08:16.290 --> 01:08:18.000
So they never think that there's a reality

01:08:18.000 --> 01:08:19.710
outside of the news they follow,

01:08:19.710 --> 01:08:21.570
and they're so shocked, and so judgemental

01:08:21.570 --> 01:08:22.650
when they see that news,

01:08:22.650 --> 01:08:25.860
but that's your fellow
citizens who think differently.

01:08:25.860 --> 01:08:29.430
And I think the real crisis is, for me,

01:08:29.430 --> 01:08:32.010
is that if you can't create a consensus

01:08:32.010 --> 01:08:35.250
on certain basic universal
values in public policy,

01:08:35.250 --> 01:08:37.650
in public health policy,

01:08:37.650 --> 01:08:40.650
a bureaucrat is meant to execute
like the migrant workers,

01:08:40.650 --> 01:08:44.460
it shouldn't matter whether
you vote BJP Congress,

01:08:44.460 --> 01:08:47.730
Tranemo, TRS, it shouldn't matter.

01:08:47.730 --> 01:08:51.030
It cannot matter at this time.

01:08:51.030 --> 01:08:55.980
And that ideological battles today

01:08:55.980 --> 01:09:00.510
obstruct partnerships in policy making

01:09:00.510 --> 01:09:02.940
is something that deeply concerns me

01:09:02.940 --> 01:09:04.740
because honestly people
who work in this space

01:09:04.740 --> 01:09:05.880
have to work with all governments.

01:09:05.880 --> 01:09:09.240
They can't afford to bring
their political leanings

01:09:09.240 --> 01:09:12.090
to being public policy
or public health experts.

01:09:12.090 --> 01:09:14.220
A doctor who heads all
their medical institute

01:09:14.220 --> 01:09:17.603
is not going to say, I vote
this way, so I will now not,

01:09:17.603 --> 01:09:18.510
you know what I mean.

01:09:18.510 --> 01:09:21.180
So, when we talk about news consumption,

01:09:21.180 --> 01:09:22.663
I mean I will follow
up what you've told me,

01:09:22.663 --> 01:09:25.680
but I will briefly also
say that please do engage

01:09:25.680 --> 01:09:27.940
with news outside of the news
that you already agree with.

01:09:27.940 --> 01:09:30.057
- [Audience Member] I
do. I watch news here.

01:09:30.057 --> 01:09:32.160
- And it's important.

01:09:32.160 --> 01:09:34.137
Honestly, it's important.

01:09:34.137 --> 01:09:34.970
- [Audience Member] It's
important to know that.

01:09:34.970 --> 01:09:36.131
- Yeah, it is.

01:09:36.131 --> 01:09:38.548
(indistinct)

01:09:42.690 --> 01:09:44.940
- I think there's a
couple of questions here.

01:09:47.400 --> 01:09:48.429
- [Audience Member] I
can just (indistinct).

01:09:48.429 --> 01:09:49.262
- Yeah, yeah, go ahead.

01:09:49.262 --> 01:09:50.095
- [Audience Member] Thanks you, Barkha,

01:09:50.095 --> 01:09:50.928
for coming once again.

01:09:50.928 --> 01:09:51.763
- Thank you.

01:09:51.763 --> 01:09:52.980
- [Audience Member] I just had a question.

01:09:52.980 --> 01:09:55.370
You touched upon education a little bit.

01:09:55.370 --> 01:09:56.530
- [Audience Member] Here is a mic.

01:09:56.530 --> 01:09:57.420
- [Audience Member] Give him a microphone.

01:09:57.420 --> 01:09:58.467
- Thank you.

01:09:58.467 --> 01:10:00.300
You touched about the pandemic

01:10:00.300 --> 01:10:01.710
like education during pandemic,

01:10:01.710 --> 01:10:03.930
and how education was
one of the last things

01:10:03.930 --> 01:10:06.090
that were opened up in India when U.S.

01:10:06.090 --> 01:10:09.210
and UK probably did it like
six, seven months before.

01:10:09.210 --> 01:10:11.510
So as you were traveling
particularly in 2021,

01:10:12.420 --> 01:10:15.120
did you see people on the
ground talking about it?

01:10:15.120 --> 01:10:16.290
Did you see any concerns

01:10:16.290 --> 01:10:18.720
about educational institutes being closed,

01:10:18.720 --> 01:10:22.560
and particularly with the
birth of mobile phones

01:10:22.560 --> 01:10:25.860
or laptops so that they
can attend virtual classes?

01:10:25.860 --> 01:10:27.210
What did you see on the ground about?

01:10:27.210 --> 01:10:30.690
- Oh, I saw in particular
lower income groups

01:10:30.690 --> 01:10:33.450
people desperate for schools
to reopen, desperate.

01:10:33.450 --> 01:10:34.950
Like there was a kind of desperation,

01:10:34.950 --> 01:10:37.320
and the one thing that actually

01:10:37.320 --> 01:10:42.320
there was a kind of class
unison was please open schools,

01:10:42.750 --> 01:10:44.760
obviously for the poor, as I said,

01:10:44.760 --> 01:10:46.020
with the child marriages and so on,

01:10:46.020 --> 01:10:47.910
the costs were different.

01:10:47.910 --> 01:10:49.470
But I think you now have enough data

01:10:49.470 --> 01:10:51.990
on the fact that children's
cognitive skills, social skills,

01:10:51.990 --> 01:10:53.520
emotional skills, learning skills,

01:10:53.520 --> 01:10:56.730
all of them have been really
sort of egregiously hit

01:10:56.730 --> 01:10:57.840
in the last two years.

01:10:57.840 --> 01:11:02.250
The other thing I saw was poor families

01:11:02.250 --> 01:11:03.810
were really dipping into savings

01:11:03.810 --> 01:11:05.640
to pay the fees of private schools.

01:11:05.640 --> 01:11:07.980
And those fees didn't automatically stop.

01:11:07.980 --> 01:11:10.230
So what was really happening
was this big crisis

01:11:10.230 --> 01:11:11.340
where incomes had halted,

01:11:11.340 --> 01:11:12.720
but the school fees didn't have to be paid

01:11:12.720 --> 01:11:15.120
otherwise you would lose
your spot in the school.

01:11:15.120 --> 01:11:17.040
And among the more,

01:11:17.040 --> 01:11:17.880
it just came to me

01:11:17.880 --> 01:11:19.890
that one of the most
tragic stories I reported

01:11:19.890 --> 01:11:21.360
was a young girl who was the daughter

01:11:21.360 --> 01:11:24.510
of an auto rickshaw driver from Hyderabad,

01:11:24.510 --> 01:11:26.670
and he had put together everything he had,

01:11:26.670 --> 01:11:27.720
sold the only piece of land.

01:11:27.720 --> 01:11:28.620
He had two daughters,

01:11:28.620 --> 01:11:31.380
and he couldn't afford
to educate them both.

01:11:31.380 --> 01:11:32.730
So he had to take this decision

01:11:32.730 --> 01:11:34.950
that I will invest my savings in the one

01:11:34.950 --> 01:11:37.200
who seems to show more scholarly promise.

01:11:37.200 --> 01:11:39.630
So he managed to get her
into Lady Shri Ram College,

01:11:39.630 --> 01:11:41.460
which is a premier college in Delhi.

01:11:41.460 --> 01:11:45.480
And when it was announced
that classes had to go online,

01:11:45.480 --> 01:11:48.513
she was so distraught because
she didn't have a laptop,

01:11:49.601 --> 01:11:51.600
and she took her whole life.

01:11:51.600 --> 01:11:56.010
And so I think we underestimate
what education means,

01:11:56.010 --> 01:11:59.460
especially to those, I mean,
I know that there were kids

01:11:59.460 --> 01:12:00.630
who had to be pulled out of school

01:12:00.630 --> 01:12:02.040
because their parents had lost their job,

01:12:02.040 --> 01:12:05.610
and they suddenly couldn't
afford to pay the fees.

01:12:05.610 --> 01:12:06.810
Fees was a major issue.

01:12:06.810 --> 01:12:09.840
So this is again an
example of a disconnect.

01:12:09.840 --> 01:12:11.460
This is what people cared about,

01:12:11.460 --> 01:12:13.263
but this was not on the media.

01:12:14.490 --> 01:12:16.170
It was just not on the media.

01:12:16.170 --> 01:12:18.450
And everywhere people would
be like, can you please,

01:12:18.450 --> 01:12:21.150
like we are just desperate for women

01:12:21.150 --> 01:12:24.390
who wanted to go back to work
to those hours in the day

01:12:24.390 --> 01:12:26.670
where your children went to school

01:12:26.670 --> 01:12:29.250
was also about your space to do something

01:12:29.250 --> 01:12:32.340
or for poorer families, the
absence of a midday meal scheme,

01:12:32.340 --> 01:12:33.900
hot one cooked meal.

01:12:33.900 --> 01:12:36.840
So, we have to join the dots
between all of these things.

01:12:36.840 --> 01:12:41.190
So I picked up a lot of
this, but it was unmovable.

01:12:41.190 --> 01:12:43.740
You could not move
policy across government.

01:12:43.740 --> 01:12:46.980
You could not, and by
the way in many cases

01:12:46.980 --> 01:12:49.470
parents of privileged classes,

01:12:49.470 --> 01:12:51.690
some were very in favor of opening,

01:12:51.690 --> 01:12:52.523
some were very opposed to it.

01:12:52.523 --> 01:12:55.367
And in the absence of that
consensus mean no schools open.

01:12:57.850 --> 01:12:59.820
- [Zara] Okay, we have an online question,

01:12:59.820 --> 01:13:01.110
and this may be our last question

01:13:01.110 --> 01:13:03.120
'cause we're shorter on time.

01:13:03.120 --> 01:13:04.530
So the question is,

01:13:04.530 --> 01:13:06.030
can you comment on the similarities,

01:13:06.030 --> 01:13:09.060
and differences between
covering COVID and covering war?

01:13:09.060 --> 01:13:11.070
- Oh, that's a great question.

01:13:11.070 --> 01:13:13.830
And I actually, if I
had to bookend my life,

01:13:13.830 --> 01:13:14.940
not that I plan to retire,

01:13:14.940 --> 01:13:18.210
but if I had to bookend
my life in journalism

01:13:18.210 --> 01:13:20.093
it would be from Kargil to COVID.

01:13:20.934 --> 01:13:24.030
Both were the toughest
stories I've ever reported.

01:13:24.030 --> 01:13:26.403
Both transformed me personally.

01:13:29.037 --> 01:13:30.180
When I went to cover war,

01:13:30.180 --> 01:13:31.650
I had to really fight as a woman

01:13:31.650 --> 01:13:34.230
to be allowed to the War front.

01:13:34.230 --> 01:13:36.030
There were no mobile phones at that time.

01:13:36.030 --> 01:13:41.030
There was no broadcast living,
capacity to broadcast live.

01:13:42.540 --> 01:13:44.280
We would still film on tape.

01:13:44.280 --> 01:13:46.710
We would walk kilometers in the mountains

01:13:46.710 --> 01:13:50.220
to find chopper pilots who
could take our tapes back.

01:13:50.220 --> 01:13:53.073
It was obviously life
and death, very risky.

01:13:54.270 --> 01:13:57.900
And yet again, my attempt
there was to humanize,

01:13:57.900 --> 01:14:00.450
and understand the psychology
of the Indian soldier

01:14:00.450 --> 01:14:03.030
who was really 24, 25 years old.

01:14:03.030 --> 01:14:06.090
And I wanted to understand
what a young man,

01:14:06.090 --> 01:14:07.680
they were all men then,

01:14:07.680 --> 01:14:11.070
infantry continues to
be all men even today,

01:14:11.070 --> 01:14:12.600
what a young man thinks

01:14:12.600 --> 01:14:16.023
when he's walking into possible death.

01:14:17.709 --> 01:14:20.550
How does courage and
vulnerability coexist?

01:14:20.550 --> 01:14:23.640
So it was kind of very
transforming for me personally.

01:14:23.640 --> 01:14:26.430
And among the toughest, scariest,

01:14:26.430 --> 01:14:29.973
riskier stories I had ever
done till it came to COVID.

01:14:30.840 --> 01:14:33.390
Where too, there was so much unknown.

01:14:33.390 --> 01:14:36.210
And in Kargil at least you knew

01:14:36.210 --> 01:14:38.220
that there were Pakistani intruders

01:14:38.220 --> 01:14:39.690
sitting on the Indian mountain peaks,

01:14:39.690 --> 01:14:41.190
and they were going to bomb you,

01:14:41.190 --> 01:14:43.200
and you had to hide when the bomb came,

01:14:43.200 --> 01:14:44.160
and you had to take cover.

01:14:44.160 --> 01:14:45.480
And I didn't always know what to do,

01:14:45.480 --> 01:14:49.533
I was very young, but I
learned certain things.

01:14:50.798 --> 01:14:52.110
I had a female producer with me

01:14:52.110 --> 01:14:55.320
when we were traveling
across India for COVID.

01:14:55.320 --> 01:14:58.980
And I don't notice because
I've done this for so long

01:14:58.980 --> 01:15:00.990
that there are no bathrooms
for women along the way.

01:15:00.990 --> 01:15:04.030
Like I'm just picking up something small

01:15:04.030 --> 01:15:05.467
where we're talking about
the gendered experience,

01:15:05.467 --> 01:15:07.020
and gender experience of reporting.

01:15:07.020 --> 01:15:08.929
So men can take a leak
wherever they want to,

01:15:08.929 --> 01:15:11.790
but there's nothing, and
she was just modified.

01:15:11.790 --> 01:15:15.660
And because I had been trained in this,

01:15:15.660 --> 01:15:18.270
I had done this in Kargil,
there were no clothes,

01:15:18.270 --> 01:15:20.130
there were no bathrooms,
there was no food.

01:15:20.130 --> 01:15:23.550
So in a way I had been
prepared for this moment,

01:15:23.550 --> 01:15:25.680
possibly because I had reported war,

01:15:25.680 --> 01:15:28.143
but this was longer than war.

01:15:29.190 --> 01:15:30.420
This was just longer.

01:15:30.420 --> 01:15:34.050
And it reminds me, actually,
I'll end with that story,

01:15:34.050 --> 01:15:37.110
it reminds me of a nurse that
I met in a hospital in Bombay.

01:15:37.110 --> 01:15:41.580
And on 26\11, which was India's 9\11,

01:15:41.580 --> 01:15:44.670
when terrorists came into
Bombay, the city of Bombay,

01:15:44.670 --> 01:15:45.990
and there was a three day siege,

01:15:45.990 --> 01:15:48.690
two of them entered
this hospital in Bombay.

01:15:48.690 --> 01:15:51.060
And this nurse had been on neonatal duty,

01:15:51.060 --> 01:15:52.470
so she was on duty in a war

01:15:52.470 --> 01:15:54.840
where there were all these
new mothers and their babies.

01:15:54.840 --> 01:15:57.930
And when these terrorists came
in with their assault rifles,

01:15:57.930 --> 01:15:59.580
she switched off all the lights,

01:15:59.580 --> 01:16:00.720
and she had the presence of mind

01:16:00.720 --> 01:16:03.453
to take away all the phones,

01:16:05.208 --> 01:16:09.000
and turn them off, and
somehow managed to save lives.

01:16:09.000 --> 01:16:10.350
And then she was on COVID duty.

01:16:10.350 --> 01:16:12.250
And I remember meeting her and I said,

01:16:14.317 --> 01:16:15.150
"Which is tougher."

01:16:15.150 --> 01:16:17.370
And she said, "Look, there
are apples and oranges,

01:16:17.370 --> 01:16:20.430
but this is so long, I
think it'll ever end,

01:16:20.430 --> 01:16:22.080
at least that night ended."

01:16:22.080 --> 01:16:22.913
Right?

01:16:22.913 --> 01:16:25.410
And I think for me

01:16:25.410 --> 01:16:28.440
both are the most meaningful
work that I've done,

01:16:28.440 --> 01:16:30.420
both are the scariest work I've done,

01:16:30.420 --> 01:16:32.100
both have transformed me personally

01:16:32.100 --> 01:16:34.110
at different stages in my life,

01:16:34.110 --> 01:16:36.060
but both teach you the same lesson.

01:16:36.060 --> 01:16:37.410
What is the job of a journalist?

01:16:37.410 --> 01:16:39.390
The job of a journalist
is to go to a place

01:16:39.390 --> 01:16:41.670
that your audience cannot access

01:16:41.670 --> 01:16:45.120
to ask the questions that
your audience wants to know,

01:16:45.120 --> 01:16:47.130
but may have nobody asking,

01:16:47.130 --> 01:16:50.620
but also to ask the questions
that nobody else is asking.

01:16:50.620 --> 01:16:52.860
And you have to do all
three and be that bridge.

01:16:52.860 --> 01:16:55.890
And therefore the lesson from
both stories is the same.

01:16:55.890 --> 01:16:57.483
That is my role as a reporter.

01:17:00.630 --> 01:17:01.572
Thank you.

01:17:01.572 --> 01:17:04.572
(audience applauds)

01:17:08.640 --> 01:17:09.473
- Thank you, Barkha.

01:17:09.473 --> 01:17:11.640
That was an amazing hour and a bit,

01:17:11.640 --> 01:17:13.440
and I'd invite everyone to come outside,

01:17:13.440 --> 01:17:14.840
and join us to talk to Barkha

01:17:14.840 --> 01:17:17.070
in a more informal setting now.

01:17:17.070 --> 01:17:18.420
- Thank you everyone for coming,

01:17:18.420 --> 01:17:20.220
and thank you to everyone online.

01:17:20.220 --> 01:17:21.053
Thank you.

01:17:21.053 --> 01:17:23.867
(audience applauds)

